window.SSS_CHAPTERS=(window.SSS_CHAPTERS||[]).concat([{"number":51,"title":"Chapter 51: Investigation [1]","paragraphs":["Samantha sat behind a wide desk of dark alloy. Behind her a floor-to-ceiling window looked out over the entire sprawl of Bloomingdale—neon streets, layered traffic, and the distant glow of the Dimensional Gate district. The moment Rael stepped fully inside, Susan closed the door with a soft but final click. She remained in front of it, blocking the only exit.","Rael felt their mana at once.","Two distinct Master tier presences filled the room. The last time he had been near Samantha he had sensed she was strong; but now, after his own awakening, the difference was impossible to miss. Each of them stood three full tiers above him. In their eyes he was little more than an ant—something that could be crushed without effort or consequence. The stronger of the two auras belonged to Samantha she looked close to Grandmaster Rank. It pressed against his skin like cold weight.","He forced a polite smile. \"Samantha. I didn’t know you worked for the Association.\"","Susan’s head snapped toward her sister. \"You know her?\"","Samantha’s expression stayed perfectly flat. \"No. We don’t.\"","Rael caught the warning in her eyes and adjusted without missing a beat. \"Right. We don’t.\"","Susan turned on Samantha at once. \"Hey. Turn off your aura. Who are you trying to flex on and tell me how did you know him?\"","\"You don’t get to talk to me like that,\" Samantha replied, voice sharp. \"I’m your superior here, and I’m the older sister.\"","\"Some older sister,\" Susan shot back. \"The one who ran away from her own assignment and dumped it on me.\"","They were at each other’s throats in seconds, voices overlapping, old arguments clearly rising to the surface. Rael stood between them with two pastry boxes still in his hands, completely lost. He had no idea why he had been brought to the top floor, why Samantha held such a high post, or why the two of them seemed ready to start a real fight in front of a stranger.","He cleared his throat. \"Excuse me. Could you tell me why you called me here?\"","Both women stopped. Their attention locked onto him at the same time. The combined pressure of their auras spiked. Rael’s lungs tightened.","\"Really,\" he managed, \"could you turn the auras down... ma’am whose name I apparently don’t know?\"","Samantha’s gaze hardened. The playful edge that had been present during the sibling argument vanished. \"Was it you?\"","Her tone had become deadly. One wrong answer and he could feel the bloodlust waiting behind it.","Rael kept his voice level. \"Could you explain what you mean by that?\"","Susan pushed off from the door, walked to the desk, and sat on the edge of it. The motion made her posture shift; Rael registered the way she settled her weight and the line of her body without meaning to. The observation felt strangely out of place, and he wondered for a brief moment whether the sealed Intelligence or simple hormones were to blame. He set the thought aside.","Susan leaned forward slightly. \"Were you the one who killed all the awakeners inside the Dream Realm in this city?\"","The question landed like a blow. They already knew people had died in the First Realm. Rael had no clear idea how much he should reveal to two Master tier Association officers who were openly suspicious of him.","He answered with a single, flat word. \"No.\"","The courtesy he normally used with powerful people dropped away. In that moment he spoke as if rank did not exist. Susan’s mouth curved in a small, interested smile.","Samantha continued, quieter but no less intense. \"Do you know what happened inside the Dream Realm?\"","\"Why should I trust either of you?\"","Susan’s hair lifted slightly as heat and mana gathered around her. \"Don’t test me, boy. I like the confidence, but there is a line between confidence and arrogance. Cross it and you will regret it. Answer the question.\"","The pressure increased until Rael’s bones felt the strain. Old noble pride kept his spine straight even while the air tried to force him down. He memorized the feeling and filed it away with a quiet promise that one day the balance would be different.","Samantha’s voice cut through the tension, carrying clear authority. \"Susan.\"","Susan turned, still irritated. \"What?\"","\"Back off. Now.\"","Susan clicked her tongue but obeyed, sliding off the desk and dropping into a side chair with open reluctance.","Samantha waited until the heavier aura receded, then spoke again, tone carefully measured. \"I’m sorry about that. We need answers, and we need them to be truthful. You can trust us.\"","She paused, then formally introduced them both.","\"My name is Samantha Ross. I currently serve as Regional Commissioner for this city. The woman beside me is Susan Ross, my sister and also a member of the Awakener Association.\"","The surname hit Rael like a second shock. Ross. The name his family had spoken with open hatred for years. Victor Ross—High Chancellor of the country, leader of the Awakener Association, the man who answered directly to the king and whose entire purpose was to keep the noble and sovereign houses in check. The nobles called him the bane of their existence. If these two were truly his daughters, or at least connected to him, then the risk of speaking was lower than it first appeared.","Still, Rael had never met them before. They were older—closer to ten years his senior—and he had no memory of their faces from university or family gatherings. He remained cautious.","\"Can I trust you?\" he asked.","Samantha’s expression softened with genuine relief. \"Yes. You can.\"","She leaned forward slightly. \"First question. Were you part of the awakening four months ago?\"","Rael nodded once.","\"Do you know what happened to the people who never came out of the First Realm?\"","He considered the words carefully, then answered. \"Yes.\"","Samantha’s breath caught. \"Are any of them still alive?\"","\"No,\" Rael said. \"They’re all dead.\"","Silence filled the cold office.","Samantha’s next question came quieter. \"Can you tell me who killed them?\"","Rael met her eyes without flinching.","\"Her name is Aurelia Crimsonvale. Heir of the Crimsonvale family.\"","---"]},{"number":52,"title":"Chapter 52: Investigation [2]","paragraphs":["Chapter 52","Both women went still.","The name Aurelia Crimsonvale landed between them like a stone dropped into deep water. They had known a noble house was involved in the First Realm deaths; they had not known which one, or how deeply. Samantha recovered first.","\"Can you prove what you’re saying?\"","\"No,\" Rael answered. \"I only saw her do it.\" He paused, then added, quieter, \"She killed people I knew. Friends.\"","He reached into his storage ring and drew out the simple necklace Lila had pressed into his hand. The metal was dull under the office lights. \"She gave me this. Told me to return it to her sister if anything happened to her.\"","The pain in his voice was unguarded for a moment. Both sisters heard it. Samantha’s expression tightened; Susan’s usual sharp edge softened by a fraction.","Rael closed his fingers around the necklace and looked at them directly. \"There’s one more thing. If you’re on their side you’ll probably try to kill me for saying it, but I don’t care anymore. Aurelia is a ghoul. I watched her eat the brains of the people she killed.\"","Neither sister looked surprised.","Rael’s eyes narrowed. \"You already knew.\"","Samantha did not deny it. \"Yes. We knew the Crimsonvales are ghouls.\"","Anger flared hot and immediate. \"Then why has no one done anything? How does a family of ghouls get to live at the center of the kingdom? How do you let that continue?\"","Susan answered, irritation clear in her voice. \"The king authorized their residence. We don’t have the authority to go against that order.\"","The words settled heavily. They answered directly to the crown. Whatever trust the Ross name had briefly earned in Rael’s mind shrank again. He was suddenly very glad he had never given his real identity.","\"That’s everything I know,\" he said flatly. \"It doesn’t look like you’re going to act on it anyway.\"","Samantha’s face showed a flicker of genuine regret. \"I apologize. I understand how this must look to you. But we truly cannot move against a direct order from the king.\"","Rael only shrugged. The moment a sovereign house was named, the Association had begun calculating political cost instead of justice. He had expected nothing better.","\"Can I leave now?\"","Samantha saw the respect she had lost in his eyes and felt a small, uncomfortable knot form under her ribs. She reached into a drawer of the desk. \"Wait. Take your identification first.\"","She held out a thin black card.","[Name: Rael Ashborne","Age: 16","Rank: E-Rank","Unique No: 2828420]","Samantha’s gaze caught on the age. Her pupils tightened. The numbers seemed to rearrange themselves in her mind. Sixteen. The morning in the apartment, the assumptions she had made, the missing clothes, the way she had apologized—every piece slammed back into place at once she slept with a teenager. She stared at the card, then at the boy in front of her, thoughts grinding to a halt.","She said \"Wait you lied about your age right?\"","Rael, already in a foul mood, simply took the card from her unresisting fingers. \"No, I didn’t lie about my age.\"","He had no idea what was running through her head.","He turned toward the door. Neither sister tried to stop him. At the threshold he paused and looked back.","\"One more thing. I think she’s still inside the city.\"","Samantha and Susan exchanged a glance. \"We’ll look into it,\" Samantha said.","Rael gave no reply. The door closed behind him.","Silence held for several seconds.","Susan dropped into the chair Rael had used and stretched her legs out. \"He was hiding something, Sam.\"","\"I know,\" Samantha answered. \"But what he did tell us was the truth. We already knew ghouls were active in the region. Now we know why.\"","\"What do we tell Father?\"","Samantha’s expression hardened into the version of herself Susan saw only rarely—the one that surfaced when she decided someone else’s suffering would not be ignored. \"Tell him to put those meat-suckers on a tighter leash. If he doesn’t, there will be a war against them whether he’s involved or not.\"","Susan knew that tone. Samantha would not let the Crimsonvales walk away clean. \"Order a full city sweep,\" Samantha continued. \"Clean out every ghoul we can find. If their precious daughter dies in the process, we’ll have all the justification we need.\"","Susan’s mouth curved. \"I’ll handle the sweep myself.\"","She leaned back, still turning Rael’s face over in her mind. Something about him felt familiar, but the memory refused to surface. Her recollection of people had always been unreliable.","\"How did you know him?\" she asked suddenly.","Samantha’s cheeks colored. \"I didn’t.\"","Susan studied the reaction and gave a short, knowing laugh. \"Liar.\"","Outside, Rael walked through the upper corridors of the Association with the new ID card in his pocket. Irritation still burned under his ribs. The Crimsonvales would face little more than a quiet warning. That was the reality of power: when the cost of justice threatened open conflict between the Association and a sovereign house, the death toll of ordinary people became an acceptable calculation.","He looked down at the card once more. Despite everything, a small, genuine smile pulled at the corner of his mouth. He was an official awakener. The thing he had wanted for as long as he could remember was finally real. His journey started here.","In the parking level he swung onto the CoreMotion bike, sealed the helmet, and pulled out into the night traffic. The knowledge that someone had already tracked his old phone sat uncomfortably at the back of his mind. It would not take them long to start hunting the new identity if they were determined. For a moment he considered returning upstairs and asking the Ross sisters for protection. The idea died almost immediately. Their reaction to the Crimsonvale name had shown him exactly how far they were willing to go.","There was only one reliable path left.","He needed to grow stronger, and faster than anyone expected. The Dream Realm was still the only place that could give him that strength.","Rael leaned forward, opened the throttle, and aimed the bike toward home.","---"]},{"number":53,"title":"Chapter 53: Neighbour [1]","paragraphs":["By the time Rael reached the apartment building the sky had already turned deep blue. Streetlights were coming on in uneven rows, and the air carried the first real chill of evening. He parked the bike in its assigned spot, locked the helmet to the frame, and rode the lift up to the nineteenth floor with the second pastry box still in his hand.","He paused in front of his own door, then stepped one pace sideways and knocked on the neighbor’s.","The woman opened it with a neutral, almost guarded expression, as if she were already preparing to turn away a salesman or a nuisance. For a second Rael wondered whether coming over had been a mistake. That morning she had been polite; now the look she gave him was closer to mild annoyance.","He held up the box. \"Hi. I ended up with an extra pastry and... today was kind of a big day for me. I thought you might like it.\"","The shift in her face was immediate. Surprise replaced the guarded look. She accepted the box with both hands.","\"Oh. Thank you.\"","Rael only smiled, gave a short nod, and went into his own apartment. Behind him he felt her eyes follow him until the door closed. He still did not understand how a person’s mood could swing so quickly from distant to soft, but he decided it was not worth overthinking. One small gain from the day mattered more right now.","When he had taken the ID card from Samantha’s hand he had quietly copied her class. A Regional Commissioner at Master-tier almost certainly held something powerful. He opened the system window the moment he was alone.","[Class detected]","[Class: Frostheart (S-Rank)]","[Do you want to copy?]","[Y/N]","He chose yes.","[System Analysis Complete.]","[New Class Detected: Frostheart]","[Original Class Rank: S-Rank]","[Warning.]","[Host’s understanding of the class is insufficient.]","[Class Rank has been adjusted according to the Host’s current understanding.]","[S-Rank → A-Rank]","[Current Class: Frostheart (A-Rank)]","[Class potential remains: S-Rank.]","[Further understanding of the class may restore its original rank.]","This had never happened before. When he copied Aurelia’s Soulbinder the rank had stayed intact. Analyze worked differently from the old Copy class—it demanded real understanding before it would grant the full power. Even reduced, an A-Rank ice class was a significant addition.","[Frostheart — A-Rank]","Class Description:","A powerful Ice-class that creates and controls extreme frost through the user’s Frostheart Core. The class excels in offense, defense, and battlefield control.","Skills:","[Frost Bolt (Novice) — Fires a concentrated bolt of ice that freezes and pierces targets.]","[Ice Armor (Novice) — Covers the body in hardened ice, greatly increasing defense.]","[Frost Manipulation (Novice) — Allows precise control over ice, shaping it into weapons, barriers, and structures.]","Every skill sat at Novice. The class was clearly too complex for him to grasp beyond the surface. That decided his next step. Tomorrow he would return to the Dream Realm and begin forcing real understanding through use.","He looked around the apartment. Only the bedroom had been properly cleaned; everything else still wore its plastic wrap or collected dust. He at least cleared the dining table so he would have a place to eat, then went to shower. By the time he finished and dressed again, full night had settled outside the windows. He had skipped breakfast and lunch; dinner would be the only meal of the day. Instant noodles were not a long-term plan. He needed to learn how to cook properly, or at least establish some kind of workable diet.","He was reaching for the phone to order food when a knock sounded at the door.","Rael checked the security panel. The neighbor stood in the hallway holding a large lunchbox. He opened the door.","\"Hello,\" she said.","\"Hi.\"","She lifted the box slightly. \"I’m sorry about earlier—for looking annoyed. I was in a bad mood. It had nothing to do with you.\"","\"It’s fine. I understand. No harm done.\"","She held the lunchbox out. \"I made extra. You’re new here, and I figured you might not have a proper setup for meals yet. So... this is for you.\"","Rael took it, surprised. \"Thank you. Really.\"","He stepped aside, leaving the doorway open. She smiled and walked in, setting the box on the cleared table. Her eyes passed over the still-wrapped furniture, but she made no comment. When she turned back she seemed to remember something.","\"I never properly introduced myself.\" She extended a hand. \"Elena.\"","\"Rael. Nice to meet you, Elena. And thank you again for the food.\"","Her hand was warm and soft. For a brief moment his attention drifted—her figure, the mature lines of her face, the quiet composure she carried—before he caught himself and let the thought die. He offered an awkward smile instead. She was clearly older than him, and even after awakening he still felt no mana from her at all. Most likely an ordinary person.","\"I should go,\" she said.","\"All right. Thank you once more.\"","She left with a final small nod. Rael closed the door and locked it, then turned to the lunchbox. Inside he found carefully arranged portions of rice, seasoned vegetables, clear soup, and slices of tender meat. The smell alone was enough to make his empty stomach tighten.","He sat down and took the first bite.","The flavors were clean and layered in a way he had almost never tasted. Not the heavy, expensive dishes of noble banquets, and nothing like the burned monster meat of the Dream Realm. Just honest, well-made food. He ate slowly, letting the simple warmth of the meal settle the last of the day’s tension.","Soon he has to enter the Dream Realm again. Tonight, for a short while, the apartment felt a little less empty.","---"]},{"number":54,"title":"Chapter 54: Neighbour [2]","paragraphs":["Blood ran down the woman’s temple in a thick line. She held both hands up, palms outward, voice already breaking.","\"Wait—wait, I don’t know anything—\"","The man in the black suit did not answer. He drove the dagger into her stomach and twisted. Her eyes went wide. A wet sound left her throat. She tried to speak again, but only a thin thread of blood came out. The light in her eyes faded. She collapsed.","In the inner office Daniel sat on the floor with his back against the door, phone trembling in his hands. No signal. He kept trying anyway, muttering under his breath, \"Work, please, work—\"","The door exploded inward. The force threw him forward onto his hands. He scrambled around and dropped to his knees at once.","\"Please—sir, whatever you want, I’ll give it to you. Just don’t kill me.\"","The man in the suit stepped over the broken door, walked to the desk chair, and sat down as if he owned the room. He looked at Daniel the way someone might look at a stain.","\"You’re a hard man to find, Daniel.\"","Daniel kept begging, words tumbling over each other. The man continued in the same calm voice.","\"Your wife only told me where you were after I killed two of your children.\"","Daniel’s blood turned to ice. The rage that tried to rise died almost immediately under the weight of what he was hearing. Helplessness flooded in behind it.","\"My wife...\" he managed. \"Is she...?\"","The man laughed, low and pleased. \"Of course she’s dead. I had my fun with her first. She was a beautiful women and couragious enough to open her legs to save her last alive son. Made your boy watch while I opened her up. But don’t worry—your son is still alive. Last I saw he was lying in what’s left of his mother intestine.\"","Something inside Daniel cracked. Tears came without sound at first, then in hard, broken sobs. He wanted to throw himself at the man and die trying to kill him. Instead he stayed on his knees. One child might still be breathing. He had to stay alive for that.","His voice came out raw. \"What do you want?\"","\"Rael Blackthorn,\" the man said simply. \"Where is he?\"","Daniel let out a sound that was half laugh, half sob. He had lost everything for a customer he barely remembered. \"If you had just asked me, I would have told you.\"","The man smiled. \"Where’s the fun in that? Then wouldn’t have got to enjoy your beautiful wife.\"","Daniel’s last thread of control snapped. He lunged. The man caught him by the throat with one hand and lifted him easily, then held up a phone with the other. On the screen a single red button waited.","\"There’s a bomb in your house. Talk, or the last one dies right now.\"","Daniel’s legs kicked uselessly. The words tore out of him. \"New Tower Apartment—19th floor.\"","The man released him. Daniel collapsed, gasping.","\"You’ll let me live?\"","\"Of course not.\"","The man pressed the button. Somewhere across the city a muffled boom would answer. Daniel’s scream was raw and endless. Only when the man was certain the sound had broken him completely did he draw the dagger again. He did not cut deep enough to kill at once—only enough to open the neck so the blood would leave slowly.","Daniel bled out on the office floor, still trying to crawl toward a son who was already gone.","The man in the suit straightened his jacket, stepped over the body, and raised his phone.","\"We have the location.\"","A voice on the other end answered, calm and final. \"Finish the job, X3.\"","\"Yes, boss. Give me a day.\"","Morning light was already spilling across the nineteenth floor when Rael woke. He dressed quickly and decided to finish cleaning before anything else. He was on his knees wiping the last stretch of the living-room floor when the knock came.","Elena stood outside holding a cloth-covered box. He was still wearing the yellow cleaning gloves. He opened the door anyway.","\"I brought breakfast,\" she said.","\"You don’t have to do this every day.\"","\"I like cooking,\" she answered simply. \"And I like it when someone actually enjoys the food. Last night you brought the empty box back and told me it was good. That was enough reason.\"","She glanced past him at the half-cleaned apartment. \"You’re already working. Let me put this inside.\"","Rael stepped aside. She set the box on the table, then nodded toward the bathroom. \"Go wash your hands. I’ll set it out.\"","He did as she asked. When he returned she had laid out two plates—soft bread, fried eggs, and a small stack of pancakes still warm from her kitchen.","He paused. \"You’re eating here too?\"","\"If you don’t want me to, I can take mine back.\"","\"Are you kidding? I’d like that.\"","The smile she gave him was small and genuine. They sat down together. For a while the only sounds were the quiet clink of forks and the distant morning traffic far below. Rael asked light questions—how long she had lived in the building, whether she had family nearby. When the second question landed she looked down at her plate and the easy atmosphere thinned. He noticed at once and shifted topics without comment.","\"Your cooking is better than anything I’ve had in a long time,\" he said. \"I don’t know how to make anything that doesn’t come out of a packet. I’d like to learn.\"","Elena glanced up again. \"I can teach you, if you want.\"","\"That’s a big favor. I can’t ask for that.\"","\"Then pay me for the lessons,\" she said, eyes glinting with mild amusement. \"That way you won’t feel like you owe me.\"","Rael understood she had only said it to make the offer easier to accept. He smiled. \"All right, teacher.\"","The word made her laugh—soft and unexpected. \"Student it is, then.\"","They finished the meal in comfortable conversation. When the plates were empty Elena looked around the still-wrapped furniture and the half-cleaned floors.","\"I can help with the rest, if you’ll let me.\"","\"That’s too much.\"","\"It’s fine. I’ll just get my own things.\" She was already moving toward the door. \"I’ll be right back.\"","Rael stood there for a moment after she left, the apartment suddenly quieter. In two days the place felt less empty than it had any right to. He shook his head once, almost smiling, and waited for her to return.","---"]},{"number":55,"title":"Chapter 55: Neighbour [3]","paragraphs":["Chapter 55","From the rooftop of the neighboring building, X3 watched them through a long-range lens.","He had lived his entire life as a weapon. As a child his village had burned in a single night. The survivors—mostly children—were taken by men in dark coats and delivered to one of the lesser noble houses that specialized in producing tools rather than heirs. The training that followed was simple and absolute: kill or be killed. Instructors repeated an old belief until it became law—if a child developed enough understanding of a class before awakening, the class would answer them when the time came. So the children were forced to study death from every angle.","X3’s sister had been taken with him. She was the only reason he survived the early years. She whispered courage when the beatings came, shared what little food they received, and promised that one day they would leave together. Then the first real test arrived. Each child was ordered to kill. Most hesitated. X3 did not. He turned on the nearest unguarded target—his own sister—and drove the practice blade into her without warning. The betrayal in her eyes lasted only a second. After that he laughed. The sound surprised even the instructors. From that day the path was set. He killed whoever he was told to kill, and somewhere along the way he began to enjoy the work. His original name faded. The house gave him a designation instead: X3. By the time he reached Master rank he had removed targets across half the continent—generals, guild leaders, even a few minor sovereign heirs.","This time the order was different. Kill a classless boy. The request had disgusted him. He had ended lives that shook nations, and now he was being asked to erase a failed awakener who should have died of irrelevance. Still, orders were orders. He had tracked the money, broken the contractor, and finally located the apartment. Through the lens he watched the boy eating breakfast with a woman. The woman was beautiful in a quiet, mature way. X3 decided he would take his time with her first. Distorting beauty had always been more entertaining than a clean kill.","She left the apartment and stepped next door. Perfect. A few minutes of privacy with the boy, then he would handle the woman when she returned. X3 shifted his weight to move.","His left leg separated cleanly at the thigh.","There was no sound of a blade, only a thin black line that appeared in the air and vanished. Blood poured across the rooftop gravel. X3 screamed and collapsed against the low wall, staring at the empty space where his leg had been.","Elena stood a few paces behind him, expression almost bored.","\"Why are you crying like a child?\" she asked. \"Aren’t you supposed to be an assassin?\"","He tried to speak and found his voice strangely muted, as if the air itself refused to carry it farther than her ears. \"You... how? Why are you here?\"","Elena crouched, still well out of reach. \"I should be asking you that. What is a Master-rank assassin doing in my city? Who sent you after me?\"","X3 clutched at the spurting stump, panic climbing fast. \"How did you—who are you?\"","Her head tilted. \"You don’t know who I am? Then why are you here if not to kill me?\"","He kept trying to stop the bleeding. A Master-tier killer reduced to scrambling on a rooftop like a wounded animal. Elena’s gaze drifted past him, down toward the apartment where Rael had already returned to cleaning the floor, completely unaware of the blood being spilled less than sixty meters away.","Her voice lost every trace of playfulness. \"Don’t tell me you’re after him.\"","X3’s breath turned shallow. The pressure around him thickened.","She stepped closer. \"You think you can take him away from me?\"","Another black line flickered from the motion of her fingers. His remaining leg left his body. He folded, whimpering, unable even to scream properly.","Dark energy coiled around Elena in thin, almost invisible threads. X3 had hunted long enough to recognize the signature. Only one person in the current era carried that particular authority over death.","\"Witch of Calamity...\" he whispered. \"What is someone like you doing in a place like this?\"","Elena smiled without warmth. \"So you do know me. Good.\"","\"At least answer me before you finish it,\" he managed. \"Why are you here?\"","\"I’ll answer,\" she said, \"if you tell me why you were trying to kill that boy.\"","X3 swallowed blood and fear. \"His name is Rael Blackthorn. His family ordered the hit. If I were you I wouldn’t involve myself with the Blackthorns.\"","Elena’s expression flattened into something colder. \"A Blackthorn. Those black-haired bastards always did have too much confidence. Walking into my territory as if it belonged to them.\"","\"Now tell me,\" X3 forced out. \"Why are you here?\"","Elena moved her fingers once more. A final black line crossed the space between them. His head left his shoulders and landed on the gravel with a dull sound. The body followed a moment later.","She looked down at the remains without expression. \"Because I want to.\"","Far below, Rael continued wiping the living-room floor, entirely ignorant of how close death had come. He never felt the Master-tier presence, never sensed the killing intent that had been locked on him only moments earlier. The morning remained ordinary.","The front door opened. Elena stepped inside carrying a bucket and an armful of cleaning cloths, as if she had simply gone next door for supplies.","\"Well,\" she said, \"let’s start cleaning.\"","Rael looked up, still on his knees, and smiled. \"Thank you again.\"","Elena waved the gratitude away with a light gesture. \"Stop being so formal, student. It’s only cleaning.\"","She set the bucket down and began working beside him, the rooftop and its fresh corpse already forgotten.","---"]},{"number":56,"title":"Chapter 56: Aetherion - Second Realm","paragraphs":["The morning had fully passed by the time Elena finished helping him. She gathered her cleaning supplies, gave the apartment one last satisfied look, and left with a short wave. Rael stood alone in the now-clean living room and felt a quiet pride. The plastic wraps were gone, the floors shone, and the place finally felt like it belonged to someone living rather than temporary. He took a long shower, changed into fresh clothes, and only then checked his phone.","A single message waited from the Awakener Association.","They had located the address he requested.","When he registered he had left his number at the information desk and asked for any record of a girl named Lila who had entered the First Realm from Bloomingdale and never returned. An employee had taken the request higher; permission had been granted. The message contained a full name and a residential address.","Lila Green.","He had never learned her family name while she was alive. Knowing it only after her death left a strange, hollow feeling in his chest. He read the address twice, memorized it, and put the phone away. Her last request still needed to be kept.","Rael locked the apartment and stepped into the hallway. Elena was just outside, dropping a small bag of trash into the chute. She glanced over.","\"You’re going to the Dream Realm now, right?\"","\"Yes, master.\"","The title had started as a joke while they cleaned; she had answered by calling him disciple. Both of them had kept using the words since. Elena smiled at the sound of it.","\"All right. Good luck out there.\"","\"Thank you. I’ll be back soon.\"","She nodded and disappeared into her own apartment. Rael still found it hard to believe how quickly the easy familiarity had formed between them. Two days earlier she had been a stranger. Now the hallway felt less empty because of her.","He took the lift down and gone towards the parking he searched for his bike, he sat on it and rode toward the address the Association had provided. He drove for few 20 minutes. The neighborhood was only a few kilometers away—there were alot of similar looking building in rows of modest single-story houses with small front gardens. He slowed when the numbers matched, parked, and walked up the short path.","No one answered the door or the bell.","Rael had already written a letter explaining who he was, how he had known Lila, and why he carried the necklace. He folded it carefully, placed the necklace on top of the paper, and slipped both into the postbox. His fingers lingered on the metal lid for a moment.","\"Goodbye, Lila,\" he said quietly. \"I won’t forget you.\"","He returned to the bike and pulled away. A short distance down the street a girl carrying grocery bags slowed when she saw a young man leaving her house. By the time she reached the gate he was already gone. She opened the postbox, found the letter and the necklace, and began to read. Halfway through the page her hands started to shake. Tears followed, heavy and unrestrained.","Rael rode with the same sadness sitting quietly behind his ribs. For the last few days work and new surroundings had kept the memories at a distance. Delivering the necklace brought everything back. The cave, the missing eyes and legs, the final smile, the words she had spoken while dying. He had clawed his way out of the First Realm in agony. Now he was deliberately riding toward the Second.","The Official Dimensional Gate came into view as a wide, luminous circle set into the middle of a reinforced plaza. Multiple guards in Association armor stood at fixed posts. The air near the rift felt slightly thinner, charged with residual mana. Rael stored the bike, approached the administration desk, and waited for the officer to look up.","\"I want to enter.\"","\"Identification.\"","Rael handed over the new card. The officer scanned it, then asked, \"First time in the Second Realm?\"","\"Yes.\"","\"Entry fee is ten thousand credits for a yearly subscription.\"","Rael transferred the amount without comment. The officer inserted the ID into a verification slot; a moment later the card returned with a new clearance mark. \"Scan at the final checkpoint and go through. Next.\"","Rael nodded and walked toward the gate itself. The structure was not a door in any ordinary sense. It had begun as a raw dimensional rift—an open wound between worlds that monsters could pour through. When awakeners pushed the beasts back and held the threshold long enough, the edges stabilized, rounded, and became a permanent, controlled passage. What stood before him now was the mature form of that process: a glowing circular boundary humming with restrained power.","He scanned his card at the last guard post. The man on duty glanced at him.","\"First time?\"","\"Yes.\"","\"Your head’s going to spin. Don’t fight it.\"","Rael stepped through.","The world folded.","Colors separated into distinct ribbons and began to rotate around him in slow, nauseating spirals. Up and down lost meaning. His stomach clenched hard, as if he had swallowed something spoiled. A white aperture appeared ahead and pulled him forward with irresistible force. Then everything went black.","When his eyes opened again the image was blurry. It sharpened slowly. He was standing on a stone street. Behind him two guards flanked the Aetherion side of the same gate. The air was noticeably warmer. High above, two suns hung in a clear sky, one slightly larger and whiter than the other.","Rael managed three steps before the nausea won. He bent over beside the nearest building and emptied everything he had eaten that morning onto the clean stone. The guards watched with the mild amusement of people who had seen the same reaction hundreds of times.","\"First-timers,\" one of them said, not unkindly.","Rael wiped his mouth with the back of his hand and straightened. The street ahead opened into a busy town square filled with people dressed in an elegant, slightly archaic style—layered coats, high collars, polished boots, and practical armor mixed without contradiction. Shops lined the sides of the road. Conversations, footsteps, and the distant ring of a blacksmith’s hammer blended into a living noise.","He had arrived in Aetherion.","---"]},{"number":57,"title":"Chapter 57: Ashfall","paragraphs":["The moment the nausea faded, Rael understood why so many awakeners never truly returned to Titan.","The City rose around him in warm stone and living magic. Buildings of pale brick and dark timber stood three and four stories high, their upper floors linked by graceful sky-bridges of woven light. Crystal lanterns hung from wrought-iron brackets, glowing with a soft gold that never flickered. The streets themselves were paved with smooth flagstones that drank the heat of the twin suns and stayed comfortable underfoot. Everywhere he looked, people moved with purpose yet without the hard urgency of Titan’s cities. Conversations rose and fell like music—laughter, bargaining, the clink of coin, the distant ring of a hammer on enchanted steel.","It felt less like a fortress and more like a place that had decided, long ago, that beauty and safety could exist together.","A hand settled lightly on his shoulder.","\"First-timer, right?\"","Rael turned. The speaker was a lean boy about his own age with a quick, practiced smile and eyes that missed little.","\"Name’s Max. Guide work. Ten silver and I’ll keep you from walking into the wrong district or overpaying for a meal.\"","Rael calculated automatically. Ten silver equaled a thousand credits—small enough to risk, large enough to buy real information. On Titan the Association enforced order. Here the Dream Realm stretched so far that no single authority could watch every road. People survived by being useful or being strong.","\"All right,\" he said. \"But the Merchant Guild first.\"","Max grinned as if he had heard the same condition a hundred times. \"Welcome to Ashfall, then. Walk with me.\"","Two gate guards watched them leave. One muttered, \"There he goes again.\" The other only shrugged. \"Let him earn it. We’ll collect our cut later.\"","Max began talking the moment they joined the flow of the crowd, his voice easy and continuous, like a river that had worn its own path.","\"Aetherion is the Second Realm. No one has ever found its edge. Maps cover only what humans and the other races have actually walked, and those maps grow every year. Other species keep their own cities—some friendly, some willing to trade, some that would rather see us gone. Most days everyone remembers the same truth: the realm itself is the real enemy. When the monsters come in force, old grudges wait. That doesn’t mean peace is perfect. Skirmishes happen. They just end before they become wars.\"","He gestured at the architecture around them. \"Looks medieval at first, doesn’t it? Guilds, clans, open markets, people in armor and robes. You’ll find research halls that would make Titan’s universities look cautious, mage towers that grow new floors when the owners need them, workshops where a smith and a formation master argue for days over a single rune. Blacksmiths, churches, academies—everything is allowed to become more than it was.\"","They passed under a sky-bridge. Light from the crystals painted soft colors across Max’s hair. Overhead the two suns—Sol and Aera— they are also called twin suns they hung in a clear sky, one slightly larger and whiter, the other a touch gold. Their combined light made every surface warm without being harsh. Rael could see flying creatures near the suns so high up he can’t know what kind of creature is flying.","\"They stay up for two full days,\" Max said, following Rael’s glance. \"Then night falls for one full day. When the dark comes the stronger beasts push closer to the walls. No one knows why the cycle exists or what sits beyond the suns. We just live by it. Markets run longer during the double day. At night time people don’t go out of the walls. Not because it’s not allowed but because at night time the realm becomes more dangerous. Then the city changes shifts, the walls thicken with watchers, and everyone remembers they are still inside a realm that wants them dead. But you will get used to it people usually spend the night drinking and partying enjoying about they got to live for another day.\"","The street opened into a wide market square. Stalls overflowed with goods that would have caused riots on Titan: weapons with faint mana auras, armor that adjusted its fit to the wearer, potions in colors Rael had no names for, stacked crates of monster materials still faintly warm from the hunt. Food vendors grilled meat and vegetables over clean blue flames that left no smoke. The smell was rich and complicated—spices, fresh bread, the sweet edge of fruit that grew only under twin sunlight. People bargained with sharp good humor. A woman in a leather apron laughed as she pressed a sample of cured meat into a customer’s hand. A child tugged his father’s sleeve, pointing at a floating toy that changed shape every few seconds. Some people never leave this world and they but a house have childrens and stay here forever.","It was ordinary life, built inside a world of monsters, and it felt more alive than any street Rael had walked on Titan.","Max pointed to a multi-story building whose façade carried dozens of different crests. \"Adventurer Guild halls. Quests, party boards, dungeon permissions, hunting rights—everything runs through them. Clans keep private compounds, but most of the day-to-day work happens here. Blacksmiths work open forges so you can watch the metal take its final form. Academies sit in the quieter districts; you’ll hear the practice chants if we pass close enough.\"","They turned into a broader avenue. The buildings grew taller and cleaner. Flowering vines that never needed soil spilled from balconies. Small water channels ran along the edges of the street, clear enough to reflect the twin suns.","\"Noble district,\" Max said. \"The old families and the new powers both keep houses here. Money and strength buy the same stone in the end.\"","At the exact center of the city rose the Tower.","It was a single shaft of dark stone so high that the upper balconies caught a different quality of light. Banners hung from its midsection, moving in a wind Rael could not feel at street level. The structure radiated quiet authority.","\"City Lord’s Tower,\" Max explained. \"Administrative heart, military headquarters, vault, and court. The strongest recognized awakener in Ashfall usually holds the seat. There’s a public tournament, a period of open support, and final word from the Solaria Kingdom. The Tower Lord governs, commands the walls, settles blood feuds between clans before they spill into the streets, controls the military, decides who is allowed into which dungeon, and stands first in line when something big enough to threaten the whole city comes out of the dark. Bloodline helps. Strength decides. The title is not a family heirloom.\"","They walked in the Tower’s shadow for a time. Rael watched a group of young awakeners in matching cloaks argue cheerfully over a map. An older woman in scholar’s robes sat on a low wall reading a book that turned its own pages. A street musician played a stringed instrument that produced notes of pure mana; children had stopped to listen, eyes wide. No one looked starved. No one looked hunted. Even the guards at the corners stood with the relaxed attention of people who expected trouble but did not live in fear of it.","Max’s voice softened, almost proud. \"People come here thinking only of power. Most of them stay because of this. You can build a life between the hunts. You can eat well, sleep under a solid roof, watch the twin suns move across a sky that never quite looks the same twice. You can fall in love with someone who understands why you disappear for days into a dungeon. On Titan everything is regulated and taxed and watched. Here the rules are simpler. Be strong enough to keep what you claim, or be useful enough that stronger people want you around. The rest is yours.\"","They reached the Merchant Guild. The building was broad and prosperous, its doors open to a constant stream of traffic, its steps worn smooth by years of boots and carriage wheels. It stood close enough to the Tower that the City Lord’s banners were visible above the roofline.","Max stopped and stretched. \"Outside the walls is the same story you’ve already heard—monsters, ruins, contested ground, places that eat the unprepared. You know that part. Go change your credits. I’ll wait here. Pay me when you come back out.\"","Rael looked once more along the avenue: the warm stone, the crystal light, the easy movement of people who had carved a bright city out of a realm that wanted them dead. For the first time since leaving the First Realm he felt the pull of something that was not simply survival or revenge.","He understood why so many never went home.","He started up the steps of the Merchant Guild, the twin suns bright on his shoulders, and the living noise of Ashfall City rising around him like a promise.","---"]},{"number":58,"title":"Chapter 58: Ashfall Trade Centre","paragraphs":["The Merchant Guild works like a private corporation in this world. It is a mixture of all the financial activities and different businesses.","The Merchant guild name of the city was Ashfall Trade Centre it dominated the plaza the way a cathedral dominates a square. Even in a city of towers and high walls it stood apart—twelve stories of pale stone and dark timber, its façade carved with interlocking geometric patterns that caught the light of the twin suns and threw it back in soft gold. Enormous pillars framed the entrance. Arched windows ran in disciplined rows. Wooden balconies edged with delicate railings projected at regular intervals, and from every floor crystal lamps glowed with steady, shadowless light. The overall impression was of a medieval palace that had been redesigned by people who understood both commerce and magic in equal measure.","Rael paused at the foot of the wide steps and read the directory board fixed to the wall. The text was clear, practical, and written in the common script used across the explored regions of Aetherion.","1st Floor – Reception Counters","Long wooden counters where merchants registered businesses, requested trade permits, and handled ordinary transactions.","2nd Floor – Trading Stalls","Small permanent booths displaying weapons, armor, monster materials, potions, magical ingredients, food, clothing, and lesser treasures.","3rd Floor – Large Display Cases","Enchanted glass cases containing valuable artifacts and rare items under constant protective wards.","4th Floor – Transaction Counters","Staff who handled currency exchange, large contracts, and high-value purchases.","5th Floor – Magic Screens and Boards","Floating displays that updated commodity prices, monster-core values, dungeon material rates, and public trade notices in real time.","6th Floor – Contract Hall","A quieter section reserved for negotiating caravan contracts, long-term supply deals, and private commissions.","7th Floor – Appraisal Room","Specialists who identified and valued monster materials, artifacts, gemstones, cores, and other goods whose true worth was not obvious.","8th Floor – Storage Vaults","Heavily guarded chambers for the most valuable goods; access required both key and blood-signature.","9th Floor – Private Offices","Rooms used by wealthy merchants and guild officials for confidential negotiations.","10th Floor – Auction Hall","A grand chamber opened only for rare artifacts, legendary materials, and other items that demanded competitive bidding.","11th Floor – Merchant Lounge","A comfortable area of tables, food, drink, and information boards where deals were discussed in softer voices.","12th Floor – Caravan Department","Offices that organized transportation, escorts, route planning, and large shipments between cities.","Beneath the public floors lay three underground levels. Those handled the guild’s internal leadership, secure transport routing, and the movement of goods that never appeared on any public ledger. Customers were not permitted below ground without explicit authorization.","The building functioned as a private corporation formed by long-standing partnerships among the city’s major trading houses. Almost every financial service that mattered in Ashfall passed through its doors in one form or another. It was less a single business and more the beating commercial heart of the city.","Rael stepped inside. The main hall on the first floor was high-ceilinged and cool despite the twin suns outside. Crystal lamps floated near the vaulted roof, casting even light across dozens of reception counters. The air smelled of polished wood, ink, and the faint ozone of active wards. People moved with purpose—merchants in practical coats, clerks with ledgers, guards whose eyes never stopped moving. It felt orderly in a way the open markets outside did not.","There was no lift. In a realm that rejected most non-mana technology, stairs remained the reliable solution. Rael began to climb. With each floor the number of casual visitors thinned. By the time he reached the fourth the traffic had become almost entirely transactional: people carrying sealed cases, signed contracts, or heavy coin purses.","The fourth floor was a single broad hall lined with identical wooden counters. Behind each counter sat a clerk in the guild’s muted green-and-gold uniform. The atmosphere was quiet, professional, closer to a bank than a marketplace. Rael approached the nearest desk.","\"I need to exchange credits for local coin. Which counter?\"","The clerk pointed without looking up from his ledger. \"Counter six.\"","The line at counter six was short. When Rael’s turn came, a woman with neat grey streaks in her hair and ink-stained fingers regarded him over a pair of thin spectacles.","\"How much would you like to exchange?\"","\"Five hundred thousand credits.\"","She wrote the figure on a slip of thick paper and slid it across. \"Bank details here. We’ll confirm the transfer.\"","Rael filled in the information for the account he had opened under the name Ashborne. The woman took the slip, spoke a few quiet words into a mana communicator no larger than a coin, and received an answering pulse of light within five seconds.","\"Confirmed. Which denomination would you prefer—gold, silver, or bronze?\"","\"Gold.\"","She opened a reinforced drawer, counted with practiced speed, and pushed a heavy cloth bag across the counter. Fifty gold coins. Rael weighed the bag once in his hand, then slipped it straight into his storage ring. The transaction had taken less than two minutes.","He turned away. There was more to learn about the guild—appraisal procedures, caravan rates, the quiet politics of the upper floors—but he had no time for curiosity. Every peer who had left the First Realm on schedule was already stronger than he was. The gap would only widen if he lingered behind the safety of the walls. He needed equipment for travel, then he needed to leave the city and begin hunting.","On the way back down the stairs he remembered Max waiting outside. The boy’s easy smile and convenient timing had the shape of a common scam: guide the newcomer, wait until the exchange was finished, then lead him into an alley where friends were ready. Rael could not be certain, and he was still far too weak to gamble against local thugs who knew the streets better than he did. He paused on a landing, closed his eyes, and adjusted Conceal with small, precise changes—hair a shade darker, jaw slightly broader, the set of the eyes altered just enough to break recognition.","When he stepped back out into the plaza the twin suns were still high. The bag of gold coins sat safely in his ring. Max was nowhere in the immediate crowd, or if he was, he no longer recognized the face he was waiting for.","Rael turned toward the market streets. The next purchases would be practical: a proper weapon, travel rations, a map of the safer hunting zones beyond the walls. After that the city could keep its comforts. He had work to do.","---"]},{"number":59,"title":"Chapter 59: Regressor","paragraphs":["(A/N: From this point on the real story starts the one I invisioned before starting this novel so please keep reading it and comment to share your views)","---","Her world Titan was dying.","Not in fire alone. Not in a single collapse. It was dying the way a body dies when every organ fails at once—slow, inevitable, and already past the point of return.","The city that had once stretched past the horizon was almost gone. Towers that had stood for centuries lay broken across streets buried under mountains of rubble. Entire districts had vanished into craters so deep their bottoms were lost in shadow. What little remained still burned. Fire crawled through shattered windows, roared along avenues where blood ran between the cracks like dark rivers, and painted the low clouds the color of old wounds.","Among the ruins the beasts had come.","They moved without urgency, tearing apart whatever still lived. Some were scaled and vast. Others were twisted things of flesh and exposed bone. Their roars rolled between the broken walls and drowned the distant screams of the few humans who had not yet been found. A massive creature dragged a corpse through the street by one leg, leaving a long smear. She barely glanced at it. She had watched that same beast perform the same act more times than she could count.","Then the dragon crossed the sky.","Its wings tore through the smoke, casting a moving shadow over the ruins before it disappeared into the clouds. The roar that followed was one she knew by heart. The same dragon. The same path. The same timing.","Her eyes lifted higher.","There was no true sky left. Beyond the shattered atmosphere hung a planet—enormous, impossibly close. She could see its mountain ranges and the slow swirl of its oceans. Clouds moved across its face as it descended, its shadow already swallowing the far horizon. She stared without surprise. She knew the exact moment the first fragment would break free. She knew the sound it would make. She knew where the pieces would fall.","A deep rumble shook the air.","A section of the approaching planet calved away and began its long fall.","Her eyes closed for a brief moment.","Again.","The thought carried no fear. Only a bone-deep exhaustion.","She had watched this ending before. More times than memory could cleanly hold. Every cycle she had tried something new. She had changed the place she made her stand. She had chosen different people to protect. She had arrived earlier, stayed later, fought until her body gave out. She had sacrificed pieces of herself she once believed essential. None of it had mattered. The ending remained the same.","Her gaze dropped to the ground around her.","Bodies covered the street. Some were comrades who had fought at her side until their last breath. Others were ordinary people who had never held a weapon. A man still clutched the hand of a child. A soldier lay face-down beside a shattered blade. Blood had sprayed across a nearby wall in a pattern she recognized. She knew some of the faces. She had watched them die in previous cycles. She had sworn, each time, that she would save them. She had failed each time.","Her fingers twitched against the blood-soaked stone. The tremor was not fear. It was frustration so pure it bordered on despair. Her body was ruined again—half of it burned beyond use, every breath a fresh tear through her chest, blood slipping steadily from the corner of her mouth. She could not stand. She could not fight. She could change nothing.","Again.","Distant screams rose from the ruins. She knew those voices. She knew how they would end.","Her eyes began to close.","Perhaps the true weakness had never been the beasts or the falling world or even the man who always arrived at the final moment. Perhaps the weakness had always been her. She had been given chance after chance, and every time she had proven too small for the ending she wanted to rewrite.","Then—","A footstep.","A boot settled into the blood directly in her field of vision.","She opened her eyes.","Another step. Slow. Unhurried.","She did not need to raise her head. She already knew the shape of the presence. White hair. A white sword. The same calm that never changed no matter how many times the world burned.","He walked through the smoke and the bodies as if the path had been laid out for him long ago. Perhaps it had. She lifted her head anyway. Their eyes met. Behind him dragons continued to circle the burning heavens. Beasts roared in the distance. Above them the planet kept its slow, inevitable descent. The end was arriving on schedule.","For a moment neither spoke.","Then she found enough breath for a whisper.","\"Was it... necessary, Ashborne?\"","He stopped. His expression did not shift. The white sword rose without haste.","She did not try to move. She already knew the arc of the blade.","It flashed.","Her head left her body. For one stretched second the world turned upside down—burning towers, falling sky, the white figure standing over her remains. Then his voice reached her, calm and almost gentle.","\"You can’t change the end, Selene.\"","Darkness took everything.","---","Selene opened her eyes.","The first thing she registered was motion—the low vibration of a vehicle moving over uneven ground. The second was the feel of rough rope binding her wrists behind her back. She turned her head as far as the restraints allowed. Other people sat or slumped around her, all of them bound. Some were crying quietly. Some stared at nothing with the blank acceptance of those who had already decided the outcome. A few had simply gone empty, as if the will to remain present had been used up.","She tested the ropes once, then stopped. The situation was familiar down to the smell of the canvas and the particular rattle of the wheels.","How many times does this make it now?","She had lost an exact count somewhere past the twentieth repetition of this same sequence. The shock had worn away years ago. What remained was a tired clarity. She was a Regressor—someone who returned to an earlier point in time each time she died—but the stories written about people like her had never matched the reality. In those tales the protagonist could try the same approach again and again until it worked. In her experience every action carried weight. People remembered things she had not yet done. Small changes created large, unpredictable fractures. No one ever behaved exactly as they had in the previous cycle. The future was not a fixed rail she could ride until she learned the correct stops. It was a living thing that shifted under every choice.","She remained still and silent among the other captives. If she did nothing, the events that had always followed this moment would unfold once more.","Selene closed her eyes again and began the quiet work of deciding which thread to pull this time.","---"]},{"number":60,"title":"Chapter 60: Dark room","paragraphs":["The room had no windows. Dim crystal lamps bolted to the rough stone walls threw long, uneven shadows across the floor, leaving most of the space in a permanent half-dark. The air smelled of damp earth, old sweat, and the metallic tang of blood. It felt less like a building and more like a bunker carved deep underground, sealed away from any sunlight or outside noise.","Max sat in the center of the circle, wrists still bound to the broken remains of a wooden chair. The thugs around him had already worked him over thoroughly. Boots slammed into his ribs. Spit landed on his bruised face. Every time he tried to curl inward, another kick found him.","\"Please—stop—I’m sorry—\"","One of the men grabbed a fistful of his hair and yanked his head up. \"Why can’t you even bring a new boy? They’re the easiest. Fresh off the gate, still dizzy, still stupid.\"","\"I don’t know where he went,\" Max gasped. \"He walked into the Merchant Guild and never came back out the way I was watching. I thought he was an easy mark.\"","Another boot drove into his side. The chair finally gave way with a sharp crack, spilling him onto the floor. He curled tighter, coughing blood onto the stone.","At the far edge of the room a larger figure watched without rising. Ben was built like a siege engine—tall, thick through the shoulders and chest, arms corded with muscle that made the other men look undersized. He sat with one boot resting on a low crate, expression flat with boredom more than rage.","\"Enough,\" he said at last.","The kicks stopped at once. Max stayed on the floor, shaking. Ben reached into a pouch at his belt, pulled out a small glass vial of pale-red liquid, and tossed it. The vial rolled across the stone and stopped against Max’s hand.","\"Drink it, you useless trash.\"","Max stared at the health potion for a second, knowing what accepting it meant. Another debt. Another reason Ben owned him a little more completely. He still pulled the cork with his teeth and drank. The liquid burned going down, then the worst of the open cuts began to close.","\"It won’t happen again, boss,\" he rasped. \"I’ll find that bastard.\"","Ben shook his head. \"Leave him. I’m sure he knew what you were. That’s why he walked away clean. Go. Bring me more kids. Now.\"","Max pushed himself to his feet, anger still boiling under the pain. The anger had nowhere useful to go, so it settled on the boy who had slipped the net—Rael. He nodded hard once and limped toward the tunnel that led back to the surface.","Ben rubbed a hand over his face the moment Max was gone. \"What am I supposed to do with these idiots? Can’t even handle something this simple.\"","Soft footsteps approached. Alice moved through the circle of thugs as if they were furniture. She wore almost nothing—short cloth that left most of her skin bare, dark tattoos winding down both arms in intricate patterns. Despite being a full-grown woman she carried herself with a deliberate, childish lightness. She climbed onto Ben’s lap without asking and curled against his chest.","\"Come on, my Benny. Cheer up.\"","Ben’s expression shifted the way it only did for her. The hardness eased. He adjusted her weight so she sat more securely against him and rested one heavy hand on her hip.","\"I’m trying, love. These fools don’t make it easy.\"","The other men kept their eyes carefully elsewhere. Ben could skin them alive with a word, yet around Alice he turned almost gentle. They had learned not to comment.","\"Lord wants five hundred this time,\" Ben said, voice low. \"We’re not even at half. Not close.\"","Alice ran her fingers through the short hair at the back of his neck. \"Felix is bringing a new batch soon.\"","Some of the tension left Ben’s shoulders. \"Felix is reliable. Still... the number’s too high.\"","Alice turned her head toward the watching thugs. Her voice stayed light, almost playful. \"What are you all standing around for? Go bring us new products.\"","A few of them clicked their tongues in irritation, but none argued. She was weaker than any of them in a straight fight, yet she belonged to Ben. That made her untouchable. They muttered \"Yes, ma’am\" and filed out toward the tunnels.","Alice looked back at Ben, smiling. \"So? Want to?\"","\"Now?\"","\"Yes.\"","Ben was a man who solved most problems with volume and violence, but near her the edges softened. He exhaled once, then nodded. \"All right.\"","She slid off his lap, took his thick wrist in both hands, and led him toward the side passage that went to their private rooms. The corridor ran past a long row of iron-barred cells. Inside, people pressed against the bars as the pair walked by—men, women, a few younger faces.","\"Please, let us go—\"","\"Mommy I want to back to my Mommy—\"","\"Forgive us, we won’t say anything—\"","The pleas rose and overlapped, desperate and raw. Ben and Alice never turned their heads. The voices might as well have been the buzzing of insects. They continued down the passage without breaking stride, the heavy door at the end closing behind them with a dull, final sound.","---","After few days...","Felix sat slumped against a blackened wall, the last of his mana gone and blood running freely from a dozen wounds. The caravan around him was already a ruin—wagons overturned and burning, the night sky painted orange by the flames. Smoke hung thick enough to choke on. He tried to lift one hand as the figure approached, the motion weak and useless.","\"Wait— I was only following orders. Please. I already told you everything.\"","The boy stopped a few paces away and looked down at him without pity.","\"You trashes don’t deserve to live.\"","Felix’s eyes widened. \"No—no, please—\"","\"Inferno Bolt.\"","The compressed spear of infernal fire left the boy’s hand with a sound like tearing air. It struck Felix square in the face and detonated. The explosion erased his head and punched a glowing crater into the rock behind him. What remained of the caravan leader collapsed sideways into the dirt, the flames already spreading across the rest of the wreckage.","Rael stood over the body for a moment longer, the heat of the skill still rising from his palm, then turned and walked into the smoke without looking back.","Selene at some distance looked at all of this happening with confusion and said \"Well it’s the first time I’m seeing this.\"","\"Who is that boy?\"","---"]},{"number":61,"title":"Chapter 61: Market","paragraphs":["~Back to the present~","Rael stepped out of the Merchant Guild with his features already shifted under Conceal. Max was still lingering near the steps, eyes fixed on the entrance, waiting for the easy mark who had never reappeared. Rael walked past him at arm’s length. The guide never glanced twice.","Rael was a man of his word even when it gained him nothing, Rael let ten silver coins slip from his fingers as if by accident. They clinked against the flagstones. Max’s head snapped toward the sound. He waited half a heartbeat, confirmed no one was watching, then scooped the coins up with a quick, greedy motion and tucked them away. A satisfied grin spread across his face. Lucky day—extra silver and the newbie would still walk into the net later. Rael watched the small theft from the corner of his eye and kept walking. The proof was enough to say that there was greed in his eyes.","He angled toward the massive city walls. The streets grew thicker with every kind of awakener the Second Realm produced. Mages in long layered robes moved in small clusters, the hems of their garments marked with faint elemental embroidery. Towering figures who stood nearly twice Rael’s height strode past, shoulders broad enough to fill a doorway—barbarian-class fighters whose very presence parted the crowd. One man walked with a half-dozen eagles circling above him in lazy spirals; a beast tamer, most likely. Clothing and gear that would have looked like costume or cosplay on Titan were ordinary here. Every buckle, every reinforced seam, every crystal inlaid in a bracer could be the thin difference between walking home and dying in the dirt outside the walls.","Most of the foot traffic flowed in one clear direction. Rael followed. After several minutes the street opened into the market center—a wide, crowded bazaar that felt alive in a way the cleaner noble avenues never did. Stalls jostled against one another under patched canvas awnings. Voices rose in a constant layered roar: haggling, laughter, shouted prices, the sharp cry of a vendor calling attention to fresh stock. The air itself was thick with competing smells. Smoke from open grills carried the rich, gamey scent of roasting beast meat—thick cuts of horned-boar haunch turning over blue mana flames, skewers of lesser-drake flesh hissing as fat dripped into the fire, stews of mixed monster parts bubbling in iron pots. Makeshift \"hotels\"—little more than open-sided tents with a few benches—served the meat on wooden trenchers to anyone with coin. The smell was heavy, primal, and strangely appetizing after the clean sterility of the guild halls.","Further in, the bazaar offered everything an awakener might need and a fair number of things no one needed. Crates of dried rations sat beside baskets of glowing fruit. Potion vendors shook cloudy vials to demonstrate their clarity. A woman sold maps that claimed to mark safe routes through the nearby hunting zones; whether the maps were accurate was another matter. Prices here ran lower than in the established shops, but the risk ran higher. A careless buyer could walk away with a bargain or with a purse cut cleanly from his belt.","Rael had grown up in a house where anything he wanted simply appeared. Bargaining had never been required. The noisy, grasping energy of the market felt foreign, almost aggressive. He moved with the flow of bodies until a different sound cut through the din—the steady, ringing strike of a hammer on metal. The rhythm was precise and oddly calming. He turned toward it.","The stall was wider than most, its front table crowded with finished work: swords, axes, hammers, a few wands, and several sets of armor. Behind the counter stood a boy who looked barely into his teens. Rael knew better than to trust appearances inside the Dream Realm; age and power rarely matched the face that carried them.","\"I can help you with anything,\" the boy said.","Rael pulled his attention from the back of the stall, where the hammer still rang at intervals. \"I’m looking for light armor.\"","The boy nodded, ducked behind a hanging cloth, and called into the workshop. \"Uncle—light armor.\"","A deep, gravel-edged voice answered. \"Wait.\"","A minute later the boy returned with four different sets and laid them out on the table. All of them showed cleaner work than Rael had expected to find in a street stall this far from the noble district. He ran his fingers over the materials, testing weight and flex. Years of seeing Blackthorn family gear had taught him the difference between competent craft and careless scrap. These pieces leaned toward the former.","His hand settled on a set of matte-black armor.","[Nightfall Armor]","[Rank: Uncommon]","A lightweight black armor designed for agility and stealth. Its flexible construction provides decent protection without restricting movement, making it ideal for scouts, assassins, and agile fighters.","\"How much?\"","\"One gold,\" the boy said.","Rael knew the price sat a little high, but he had never learned the art of talking a seller down. He paid without argument. While he put the Nightfall Armor, Rael’s eyes moved across the rest of the stall. More pieces lay further back on the table and hung from pegs along the side canvas—gauntlets etched with faint runes, a pair of boots that left a soft shimmer when the light caught them, a dark-metal bracer that pulsed with a slow inner glow. One sword in particular drew the eye: the blade carried a thin blue sheen and the tag beside it marked it Rare. The price written underneath made his newly exchanged gold feel suddenly modest. Even the better Uncommon pieces sat well above what he was willing to spend today.","He had the means, but not the intention. Rare and high-end Uncommon gear would drain the bulk of his coins for gains he could not yet fully use. Better to move light, learn the land outside the walls, and return with clearer needs—and clearer strength—before chasing expensive artifacts. For now simple tools would do.","He added a common-rank bow and a serviceable short sword to the purchase. Both were plain, well-balanced, and honest in their limits. A proper wand could wait; the ones on display either lacked the focus he wanted or carried price tags that belonged to a later stage of the journey. He stored everything in his ring, gave the boy a short nod, and stepped back into the moving current of the bazaar.","The noise closed around him again—the hammer still ringing somewhere behind the stall, vendors calling prices, the rich smell of roasting beast meat drifting from the open grills and makeshift hotels. Crowds pressed and parted. Somewhere ahead the massive walls of Ashfall rose against the twin-sun sky. Outside them the real work would begin.","---"]},{"number":62,"title":"Chapter 62: Gate","paragraphs":["Rael opened his storage ring and checked the contents one final time.","[Nightfall Armor — Uncommon]","[Ironfang Short Sword — Common]","[Oakwood Bow — Common]","[Minor Health Potion ×5 — Common]","[Steel Fang Dagger ×2 — Common]","[Windstep Hunter Shoes — Common]","[Beef Jerky ×20]","[Local Map ×1]","The modest collection had still cost him twenty gold coins—two hundred thousand credits in Titan terms. Health potions alone had taken a painful share of the total. He closed the ring, left the noise of the bazaar behind, and started walking toward the outer wall.","The wall did not simply rise. It dominated.","From a distance it already looked immense. Up close it became something closer to a man-made mountain. Dark stone blocks, each larger than a carriage, stacked higher than any tower in the inner city. The surface was scarred by old monster attacks and repaired with newer, smoother sections that still failed to hide the sheer age of the structure. Watchtowers jutted outward at regular intervals, tiny against the wall’s bulk. Standing at its base, Rael felt the same smallness he had felt the first time he stood beneath the Blackthorn family estate as a child—only stronger. The wall made the people moving beneath it look like insects.","The gate itself was a tunnel cut through that mountain of stone. The opening was wide enough for four heavy carriages to pass abreast and tall enough that even the largest barbarian-class awakeners did not need to duck. Twin portcullises of blackened steel hung ready to drop. Mana crystals embedded along the arch glowed with a steady white light, feeding the defensive formations that kept the gate from becoming just another weak point.","Hundreds of people flowed in both directions. Hunting parties returned with bloody canvas bundles and battered armor. Fresh groups headed out with clean gear and nervous energy. Supply carriages rattled through under heavy guard. In the middle of the traffic stood the royal knights of Solaria, unmistakable in their polished white plate. Only their faces showed. They checked papers, collected fees, and kept the line moving with the bored efficiency of men who had done the same job for years.","Rael joined the outbound queue. It moved faster than he expected. When his turn came he stood before a knight with a white-and-black beard and a completely bald head. The man’s eyes flicked over him once.","\"Business?\"","\"Hunting. I want to go outside.\"","The knight studied him a moment longer. \"First time. Why aren’t you with a party?\"","\"Is a party required?\"","\"Not required. Strongly advised for the first few trips.\"","\"I want to go alone.\"","The knight gave a short, dry laugh. \"Lone wolf, is it? Suit yourself. Try not to die too quickly. Fee is one hundred silver—yearly pass.\"","Rael paid without comment. He disliked the man’s tone, but he understood it for what it was: routine rather than personal malice. The knight scribbled on a thick slip of paper, stamped it, and handed it over. The dates ran from today to the same day next year.","\"Move along. Come back alive if you can manage it.\"","Rael stepped through the tunnel. The temperature dropped the moment he left the open plaza and entered the thick stone passage. The far end framed a slice of open land under the twin suns. He kept walking.","Just outside the gate, a small group of five had stopped to argue in low voices.","\"Our mage still isn’t here,\" a broad-shouldered young man said. He carried a round shield and looked irritated. \"He promised he’d meet us at the wall.\"","A girl with a short spear clicked her tongue. \"We waited twenty minutes already. He’s not coming.\"","\"So what now?\" the archer of the group asked. \"We can’t go out short a caster. Last time we nearly lost half the party because we had no range.\"","The leader, a lean young man with a practical short sword and calm eyes, exhaled. \"We look for a replacement or we cancel. I’m not taking us out under-strength again.\"","He glanced toward the stream of people still exiting the gate. His gaze landed on Rael—solo, newly geared, moving with the careful awareness of someone who had never set foot beyond these walls before. The leader stepped into Rael’s path and raised a friendly hand.","\"Hi. Name’s Kira. We’re short a mage for today’s hunt. We’re all still new, but we’ve got one successful trip under our belts already. You looking to join a party?\"","Rael stopped. The offer was reasonable. The composition of the group looked balanced. Few months ago he might have accepted without thinking. Now the memory of the First Realm rose unbidden—Nathan, Alex, Suzy, Lila. Especially Lila. The weight of those losses still sat in his chest like a stone and the weight of betrayal from Suzy who was Aurelia still there. He can’t trust anyone after that experience and he had no desire to carry new names into the same kind of danger.","\"I’m not a mage,\" he lied.","Kira blinked, clearly having sensed the density of mana around him. \"Could’ve fooled me. Still—any role you’re comfortable with, we can make it work. Extra blade or extra bow is better than nothing.\"","\"No.\"","Kira’s polite smile tightened a fraction. \"Can I ask why?\"","\"I don’t want to explain.\"","There was a short silence. Kira stepped aside. \"All right. Your choice. Good luck out there.\"","Rael walked on without answering. Behind him the party watched his back for a few seconds.","\"What’s with the attitude?\" the shield-bearer muttered.","The archer shrugged. \"Probably thinks the outside is a stroll. If he makes it back at all, he’ll be begging for a party next time.\"","Kira shook his head once. \"Leave it. He’ll learn the moment he’s alone past the first treeline. Come on—we still need a mage. Let’s see if anyone else is looking for a group.\"","They turned back toward the gate traffic. Rael kept walking, the massive wall rising behind him like the edge of the world, the open land of Aetherion stretching ahead under the light of two suns.","---"]},{"number":63,"title":"Chapter 63: First Journey [1]","paragraphs":["The moment Rael passed through the last of the crowd, the world changed.","Behind him the wall rose like a final argument between safety and everything else. Inside those stones were markets, warm beds, familiar voices, and the careful illusion that civilization still held the upper hand. Outside, that illusion ended within a few hundred steps.","The road simply stopped. Packed earth gave way to tall grass that swallowed the path whole. Twisted trees pressed close on both sides, their branches knitting overhead until the twin suns became broken coins of light on the forest floor. The voices of merchants and returning hunters faded faster than he expected. One moment they were a low murmur at his back; the next there was only the wind.","It moved through the leaves with a restless, continuous whisper, as if the forest itself were speaking just below the level of understanding. Rael stopped and listened. For the first time there was nothing between him and the wilderness—no party, no Lila watching the treeline while he watched the road, no one to call a warning if something moved wrong. He had told himself he did not mind being alone. Standing here, the absence felt heavier than he had prepared for.","His hand settled on the hilt of the short sword. The grip was still unfamiliar.","Somewhere far off, something screamed.","The sound was not human. It was deep, wet, and distorted, rolling through the trees before dying into silence. Rael’s shoulders tightened. He had heard worse in the First Realm. He had lived through worse. Yet those horrors had always come with other people beside him—people he could not save, in the end. The memory of Lila’s final smile flickered and he pushed it down. He had promised himself he would not lose anything that belonged to him again. The promise was still new, still sharp. It did not yet feel like armor.","He walked on.","The canopy thickened. Sunlight fell in narrow, uncertain shafts. The air grew cooler and carried the smell of damp earth and something faintly metallic. He stared into the spaces between the trunks. Nothing moved. That absence of motion felt worse than open threat. The wilderness was not empty; it was patient. Every patch of tall grass could hide teeth. Every shadow could hold a body that did not breathe the way humans breathed. The silence itself felt arranged, as if the forest were waiting to see what he would do next.","He took another step. Then another. His heartbeat sounded too loud in his own ears.","Fear was present—he did not bother denying it—but underneath it moved something else. A thin, stubborn current of excitement.","For years he had sat at family tables listening to older siblings speak of the Dream Realm as if it were a series of stories they had finished reading. They compared scars and cores and close calls while he remained outside the conversation, the child who had nothing to add. Now the stories had become ground under his boots. The unknown was no longer abstract. It was the next tree, the next sound, the next decision that might keep him alive or end him without witnesses.","He realized, after a longer stretch of walking, that he had not yet seen a single monster. Near the city the hunting pressure was constant; larger beasts rarely wandered this close. Still, Aetherion was not known for scarcity of monsters. The absence began to feel deliberate. He was being allowed to walk deeper, or something was simply choosing its moment.","A branch snapped to his left.","Rael froze. His eyes cut toward the sound. The short sword was half-drawn before he finished the turn. Nothing. Only leaves settling back into stillness. He held his breath until his lungs burned, then let it out in a slow thread. A small shape—some forest creature no larger than a cat—darted through the undergrowth and vanished. The laugh that escaped him was quiet and shaky.","\"So this is what it’s like...\"","He looked ahead. The trees continued without end. No patrols would come if he fell. No healer would step out of the brush if poison touched his blood. Every choice belonged to him alone, and every mistake would be his to carry. He had walked through the gate on purpose. He knew the reasons, even if they were still simple and incomplete: to hunt, to kill, to grow stronger, to prove he was not the useless boy the world had decided he was. And beneath those reasons, quieter and more binding, the promise he had made over Lila’s body. He would not lose what was his. Not again and for that he has to grow stronger much stronger. There are millions of people who are stronger than him right now and he has to catch up to everyone he has to beat everyone and grow stronger. If there is even one person stronger than him then he is still weak that’s his resolution, his promise to himself.","The thought should have felt heavy. Instead it settled into place like a blade finding its sheath. He was still growing. He did not yet have the clear, distant goal that older hunters spoke of with certainty. For now it was enough to take the next step, and the one after that, and to refuse to turn around.","He moved deeper.","Behind him the wall was already gone, erased by trunks and shadow. Ahead the Wildlands opened in every direction—monsters, other hunters, ruins no map fully recorded, things the people inside the walls preferred not to name. Humanity did not stand at the top of the food chain out here. The forest made that truth obvious without needing to speak it.","Rael adjusted his grip on the sword, felt the weight of the new armor against his shoulders, and kept walking. The wind continued its low conversation in the leaves. Somewhere farther in, another non-human sound rose and fell. He did not stop.","The Wildlands had noticed him.","He intended to notice them back.","---"]},{"number":64,"title":"Chapter 64: First Journey [2]","paragraphs":["Rael kept walking. The deeper silence had begun to feel wrong. No birds. No distant calls. Only the low push of wind through the canopy. He glanced back the way he had come, measuring the path against the thickening trees, and decided against pushing farther on the same line. First time out, no reason to be stupid. He changed direction, angling toward a stretch that still let a little more light through.","The forest closed in anyway.","He noticed the small beast first—the same small shape that had startled him earlier. It looked like a rabbit. It hung suspended between two trunks, wrapped in pale strands that glistened even in the dimness. The web was thick enough to hold an animal that size without effort. Strange, rope-like threads stretched from tree to tree in uneven patterns, some still quivering as if recently disturbed.","The rabbit made no sound. Its sides rose and fell in quick, panicked breaths. Rael felt no immediate threat from the creature itself. He stepped closer, crouched, and reached out to cut it free. Instinct made him shift his head at the last instant.","A clear droplet fell past his cheek and struck the ground where his hand had been.","The soil hissed and blackened. A small circle of rot spread outward, eating leaf litter and dirt alike. Rael’s eyes snapped upward.","He could hear the movement.","[Monster Detected]","Species: Venom Spider","Level: 17","Tier: Savage","The spider dropped in a single smooth motion, body the size of a grown man, legs unfolding like blades. If he had stayed still even half a second longer the leading leg would have punched through his shoulder. Rael threw himself backward, boots scraping over roots, and came up with the Ironfang short sword already drawn.","He settled into the basic guard the Blackthorn tutors had beaten into him years ago—weight low, blade angled to intercept, free hand ready to check or grab. The forms were meant for human opponents and clean dueling floors, not for something that moved on eight legs and spat poison. Still, the structure was there. Footwork. Timing. The habit of watching the shoulders—or in this case the cephalothorax—for the tell before a strike.","The spider’s abdomen clenched. Another jet of venom shot toward him. Rael dropped under it and drove forward in the same motion, trying to close distance the way the old drills demanded. Steel flashed toward one of the jointed legs. The spider was already gone. It skittered sideways with unnatural speed, claws finding purchase on bark, and vanished into the lower canopy.","Not a mindless beast. It had chosen not to trade blows.","Rael followed, sword held in the middle guard, eyes never leaving the shadows above. Leaves rustled to his right. He turned. A thick strand of web shot out like a spear. He cut across it with a short, economical slash—the same angle used to deflect a thrust in the family forms—but the impact still jarred his wrist. The web was heavier than it looked, sticky and resilient. The cut ends whipped past his face and clung to a nearby trunk.","The spider came again from a different angle, legs stabbing down in a rapid sequence. Rael gave ground in measured steps, blade turning each point aside the way he had once turned aside practice spears. Sparks of chitin and steel rang in the quiet. He managed a single counter-cut toward the body; the spider simply reared and let the blade pass underneath, then answered with another spray of venom that forced him to roll.","He came up breathing harder. The creature was faster than its size suggested and smart enough to refuse a straight fight. Every exchange pulled him a little deeper into the trees. He began to notice the pattern only after the fourth or fifth retreat: the spider was not fleeing at random. It was laying new strands as it moved, thin at first, then thicker, closing a rough circle around the clearing they kept returning to.","Rael tested the edge of that circle with a quick step outward. Fresh web stretched between two trunks at chest height, almost invisible until he was nearly on it. He sliced through, but more strands waited beyond. The spider had been working the entire time—building the ground that favored it.","He turned back to the center. The Venom Spider hung head-down from a high branch, eight eyes reflecting the thin light, legs flexed and ready. The web enclosure was nearly complete. The only clean way out now was through the monster itself.","The spider’s next strand was thicker than the others. It shot out in a tight spiral, wrapped the blade of the Ironfang short sword, and yanked with sudden, brutal force. Rael’s grip tore open. The sword flew from his hand, spun once through the air, and slammed against a nearby trunk where fresh web waited. The sticky mass closed over the steel and pinned it fast, hilt jutting out at a useless angle.","Rael stood empty-handed.","The Venom Spider lowered itself fully to the forest floor, eight eyes fixed on him. It had watched him fight with the blade; without it, the human looked finished. The creature advanced without haste, legs lifting and setting with deliberate confidence. A swordsman stripped of his sword was only meat.","It lunged.","Rael threw himself sideways between two close trunks. A leg spear punched into the bark where his chest had been. He kept moving, using the trees the way the spider had earlier—placing trunks between them, forcing the monster to climb or circle. Venom hissed past his shoulder and ate a dark hole in a root. Another strand of web clipped his ankle and nearly took him down; he ripped free and kept the distance open.","He was no longer trying to win ground. He was choosing it.","Every retreat carried him farther from the densest weave of canopy and the thickest clusters of pre-laid web. He angled toward a stretch where the trees stood farther apart and the ground was clearer. The spider followed, convinced the pattern was simple prey flight. It dropped from the branches more often now, forced to cover open space on foot. Its speed remained dangerous, but the constant high-ground advantage had begun to thin.","Rael’s lungs burned. Sweat ran into his eyes. He kept the rhythm anyway—dodge, slip, draw it another few meters. The spider’s attacks grew more aggressive as the human refused to collapse. It wanted the kill finished before the prey could find another weapon or another idea.","At last Rael’s back met the broad trunk of an old tree. The bark was rough against his armor. To the left and right the undergrowth rose thick; behind him the tree offered no path. It looked like the end of the line.","The Venom Spider slowed. It raised its two front legs high, venom beading at the tips, body coiled for the final downward strike. The eight eyes reflected the thin light. In that posture the monster filled Rael’s vision completely.","Rael smiled.","The expression was small and sharp. The spider hesitated—just a fraction, a predator’s instinct checking an unexpected signal from prey that should have been broken.","Rael’s gaze moved past the creature to the surrounding trees, then returned to the eight eyes.","\"Exactly where I wanted you.\"","Understanding arrived a moment too late. The spider’s head twitched, reassessing the ground. The dense network of web that had once enclosed the earlier clearing was now well behind them. The canopy here was thinner. The strands it had tried to set during the chase were scattered and incomplete. It had followed the human out of its prepared kill-zone and onto ground that favored neither of them equally—ground the human had chosen.","\"You are clever for a monster but that cleverness only brought you to your end.\"","Rael’s empty hands lifted. Heat began to gather against his palms, faint at first, then sharper. The air around his fingers shimmered.","The spider recoiled a step, legs spreading wide again, but the advantage it had built so carefully was already gone.","Rael’s smile did not fade.","---"]},{"number":65,"title":"Chapter 65: First Journey [3]","paragraphs":["The Venom Spider came at him the instant it decided he was finished.","Eight legs drove against the earth with enough force to tear shallow trenches in the dirt. The heavy body launched forward in a straight, killing line, front claws already angled to pin and puncture. In its clustered eyes the human was unarmed, cornered, and out of options—prey that had exhausted its only visible weapon and now stood waiting to be ended. The distance closed in less than a heartbeat.","Rael raised his right hand.","\"Inferno Bolt.\"","Fire collapsed into existence above his palm. It began as ordinary orange flame, then compressed so tightly the color bleached toward white at the core. Heat rolled off the forming projectile in visible waves. The bolt left his hand with a sound like air being torn down the middle. The spider threw itself sideways at the last possible fraction of a second. Even so the projectile clipped the edge of its carapace and detonated. The explosion lit the entire clearing in a hard, flat flash. Chitin cracked and blackened across one full flank. Smoking fragments of shell spun away into the undergrowth and landed with small, angry hisses. The monster’s voice rose from a low hiss into a higher, furious register that scraped against the trees.","It did not retreat.","Multiple spinnerets flexed at once along its abdomen. Thick strands of web shot outward in a spreading fan, timed and angled to cut off every clean line of escape. The strands were heavier than the earlier ones, designed to wrap limbs and drag a target down. Rael’s answer was already moving through his hands.","Flame Manipulation.","He swept both arms in a short, controlled arc. Fire answered the motion, rising from the ground in a curved wall that followed the path of his hands. The barrier climbed to chest height and held. The webs struck it at full speed and flashed into ash before they could reach him. Embers drifted down through the heated air like red snow. The wall of flame continued to radiate outward, forcing the spider to check its next step and turn its face away from the worst of the heat.","Rael felt the change in the creature’s posture as clearly as if it had spoken. Direct rushes had already cost it a section of armor. Web ambushes were being burned out of the air before they could land. The spider began to circle instead of charge, body low, legs testing the edges of the fire, looking for a gap or a moment when the human’s control might slip. It was measuring how long he could keep the flames alive.","He decided not to give it the time to finish that calculation.","Hellfire.","He drove the skill into the soil in a rough ring around the spider’s current position. Dark-red flames erupted in a continuous band—low at first, almost modest, then climbing as they fed on their own intensity and on everything they touched. The fire did not flare and vanish. It stayed. It clung to dirt, to roots, to fallen leaves, spreading in slow, stubborn tongues that refused to be stamped out. The spider’s legs jerked back from the heat each time they drew too close. Every direction it turned, the burning line waited. Its circle of free movement shrank with every second the skill remained active. The air inside the ring grew hotter and thinner.","The monster broke for the trees.","It needed height. It needed the canopy and the pre-laid strands it still understood. The leap toward the nearest trunk was powerful even with the damaged flank. Rael watched the trajectory and felt the second class stir under his skin—the cold, precise weight of Frostheart that he had copied and had not yet tested in a real fight.","Frost Manipulation.","He closed his left hand and pulled. Moisture latent in the air and the soil answered at once. A barrier of clear, thick ice climbed out of the ground directly in the spider’s path, jagged plates forming and locking together with audible cracks. The structure rose faster than the spider could adjust. The creature hit it at full speed. The ice shattered under the impact in a spray of sharp fragments, but the collision robbed the spider of nearly all its forward momentum. Legs slipped on the sudden debris. The heavy body staggered, abdomen dipping, balance broken for a critical moment.","Rael was already moving.","Frost Bolt.","A spear of pale-blue ice formed above his open palm and flew without pause. It struck the joint of a foreleg with a sharp, crystalline crack that carried through the clearing. Frost raced outward from the point of impact, coating chitin, racing along the limb, locking the joint in a spreading sheath of opaque white. The leg dragged when the spider tried to plant it. The next step became a pronounced limp. The creature’s entire left side dipped.","It understood the danger now. Staying at range meant being carved apart by alternating waves of fire and ice. Closing the distance was the only option that still offered a chance to end the human before more limbs were taken. The spider gathered its remaining strength, rear legs coiling, and leapt—straight at him, front legs extended, venom glistening on the tips, the full weight of its body committed to a final crushing attack.","Rael met it with the last defensive skill he had ready.","Ice Armor.","A shell of hardened frost slammed into place across his torso, shoulders, and arms a fraction of a second before impact. The spider’s claws smashed into the ice with a sound like stone striking stone. The force drove Rael backward. His boots tore deep furrows through the dirt. The armor cracked and flaked under the concentrated pressure, absorbing the worst of the penetration and distributing the rest. Pain still flared along his left arm where one tip found a seam and opened a shallow cut. Blood welled, bright against the fading white of the breaking frost.","He caught his balance, glanced once at the wound to confirm it was not deep, then lifted his eyes again.","The Venom Spider stood a few body-lengths away. Chest plates were scorched black and still smoking. One leg remained sheathed in stubborn ice that refused to crack. Its movements had become visibly heavier, each step a negotiation with injuries that were stacking faster than it could ignore. Steam rose where residual Hellfire still licked at the ground around its feet. The creature’s breath came in wet, uneven pulls. It was no longer the patient hunter that had built a careful web cage around him at the start. It was wounded, slowed, and cornered in its own turn.","Rael settled his stance. Heat waited under the skin of one hand. Cold waited under the other. Both were ready for the next command. The shape of the fight had bent fully in his direction. He could feel the end approaching—close enough to taste, not yet finished.","The spider raised its front legs once more. The motion was less fluid than before, the injured limbs trembling with the effort, but the intent was unmistakable. One last, desperate attack. Everything it had left.","Rael’s hands came up.","The clearing held its breath.","---"]},{"number":66,"title":"Chapter 66: First Journey [4]","paragraphs":["The Venom Spider gathered what remained of its strength and committed everything to one last charge.","It came in a broken, furious line—eight legs driving against scorched earth, body low, spinnerets already working. Multiple strands of web shot forward in a dense, overlapping spray timed to pin him in place while the legs followed to crush and pierce. There was no more circling, no more careful testing of distance. The monster had decided the fight would end here, one way or the other.","Rael did not give ground.","Flame Manipulation answered the first demand he placed on it. Fire coiled along his right arm, tightening, lengthening, taking the rough shape of a blade that burned without a physical edge. The heat distorted the air around his forearm. When the webs reached him he swept the burning construct across their path. The strands blackened, curled, and fell apart in drifting ash before they could wrap his limbs or chest. Embers scattered across the dirt.","A heavy foreleg scythed toward his head. Rael dropped under it, feeling the wind of the strike pass through his hair, and stepped inside the spider’s reach. For a moment the bulk of the creature loomed directly above him—the scorched carapace, the ice still clinging to one leg, the wet sound of its breathing. He did not waste the position on a shallow cut. He was already moving past, forcing the spider to turn if it wanted him in front of it again.","It turned faster than its injuries should have allowed. Another leg lashed out. Rael twisted, the burning blade on his arm scoring a shallow line across the limb as he slipped clear. The monster’s momentum carried it a half-step farther than it intended.","Frost Manipulation.","He drove the cold downward. Moisture in the soil flash-froze into a slick, uneven sheet directly beneath the spider’s weight. Six functional legs met ice instead of purchase. The creature’s stance shattered. It skidded, legs splaying, abdomen dipping toward the ground. The sound that came from it was part hiss, part snarl.","Rael was already shaping the next strike.","Frost Bolt.","The spear of pale ice formed and flew in a single breath. It struck the joint of a second leg with a sharp, crystalline crack. Frost raced outward from the impact, racing along the limb, locking chitin and tendon in a spreading white sheath. Two legs now dragged, heavy and half-useless. The spider’s posture collapsed further. It caught itself on the remaining limbs, body listing, underbelly briefly exposed as it struggled to stay upright.","Rael’s eyes caught the difference in texture—the softer, less armored section beneath the main plates of the abdomen. A vulnerability the earlier exchanges had not shown. He stepped back at once, opening distance before the monster could gather itself and close again.","The spider tried to rise. The frozen legs refused to take full weight. It staggered, adjusted, and still tried to come forward.","Rael raised his open hand.","\"Inferno Bolt.\"","The compressed projectile formed in a tight, white-hot core and left his palm with the familiar tearing sound. This time the spider had neither the speed nor the clean footing to slip aside. The bolt struck the softer section squarely and detonated. Fire punched inward. The explosion tore through internal structure with a wet, heavy rupture. Fragments of burning chitin and darker matter sprayed across the clearing. The spider’s entire body convulsed. It remained standing only by stubborn, failing instinct.","Rael walked toward it through the thinning remnants of Hellfire still clinging to the ground. The heat licked at his boots and the edges of his armor without slowing him. Flame Manipulation rose again—this time focusing the fire into a tighter, denser edge along the same arm, turning the burning construct into something closer to a true cutting weapon. He did not rush. He measured the distance, the height of the wound, the last weak movements of the legs.","The final slash was short and precise. The flaming edge drove through the already ruined core of the abdomen and out the other side. The Venom Spider’s legs folded in a progressive collapse. The heavy body settled into the dirt with a long, uneven shudder, then went still. Residual flames continued to eat at the edges of the carapace for a few seconds longer before dying down.","Around the clearing the remaining strands of web withered and burned away in slow orange lines. Smoke rose in thin columns. The sharp chemical smell of venom mixed with the cleaner scent of wood ash.","Rael stood over the corpse until he was certain no reflex remained. Then he turned, crossed to the tree where his short sword still hung trapped, and cut it free with a controlled application of flame that freed the steel without ruining the grip. He slid the blade back into its sheath and only then took stock of himself.","A few shallow cuts. Bruises that would darken by evening. The earlier slice on his arm had already stopped bleeding. Nothing that required a potion. The realization settled with quiet satisfaction—he had used every skill available to him in a single continuous fight, without holding back for an audience or a later contingency. Long-range pressure, battlefield control, defense, and a finishing close strike. The gaps were still obvious. His pure melee ability remained weak; without a proper technique the sword was little more than a tool he could swing. That would have to change. Magic could carry him only so far if he ever lost the space to shape it.","He crouched beside the spider’s ruined abdomen, grimaced at the heat and the smell, and forced his hand into the damaged cavity until his fingers closed around a hard, rounded shape. The monster core came free with a wet sound. It was smaller than some he had taken in the First Realm, yet it glowed with a steady, dense light that spoke of higher concentration. He turned it once in his palm.","\"Not bad.\"","The system registered the kill a moment later.","[+1 Level up]","[+50 Souls]","Rael closed his hand around the core and stored both it and the corpse. The fight replayed itself behind his eyes in fragments—the web cage, the deliberate retreat onto open ground, the alternating fire and ice, the final commitment. He had killed stronger monsters by pure level in the First Realm, yet few of those battles had felt this demanding. The spider had been intelligent, adaptive, and unwilling to offer simple openings. Part of the difference might have been Aurelia’s control; those earlier beasts had often moved like puppets with borrowed strings. The rest was likely the simple fact of Aetherion itself. Mana density here sat heavier in the air and in the bodies that had grown under it. Everything hit harder. Everything lasted longer.","He took a final look at the blackened clearing, then turned away. The deeper forest waited, and with it creatures that had never stepped into the thinning light near the city walls. Rael adjusted the fit of his armor, felt the comfortable weight of the recovered sword, and started walking.","Behind him the last of the webs collapsed into ash. Ahead, the Wildlands continued without comment.","---"]},{"number":67,"title":"Chapter 67: First Journey [5]","paragraphs":["Rael opened his status the moment the last of the spider’s heat left the air.","╔══════════ STATUS ═════════╗","Name: Rael","Race: Human","Level: 13 → 14","Tier: Awakened","Main Class: Analyze (E-Rank), Infernomancer (B-Rank)","Copied Class: Frostheart (A-Rank) (8%)","Bloodline: Abyssal Sovereign","Stage: Dormant","Effect: +100% Growth Multiplier","━━━━━━━━ ATTRIBUTES ━━━━━━━━","Strength: 42 → 44","Agility: 48 → 50","Mana: 46 → 48","Vitality: 42 → 44","Intelligence: 10 → 11 (Sealed)","Unallocated Stats: 8","━━━━━━━━ RESOURCES ━━━━━━━━━","Souls: 23 → 73","HP: 44 / 44","━━━━━━━━ SKILLS ━━━━━━━━━━━","Inferno Bolt (Expert), Hellfire (Expert), Flame Manipulation (Master), Frost Bolt (Novice), Ice Armor (Apprentice), Frost Manipulation (Novice)","━━━━━━ BLOODLINE SKILLS ━━━━━━━","Conceal (Grandmaster)","╚═════════════════════════╝","Skill Upgraded —","Ice Armor (Novice) → Ice Armor (Apprentice)","The numbers were higher. The difference was already noticeable in the way his body settled after the fight. Strength and vitality had climbed together; the shallow cuts felt less relevant, and the residual ache in his shoulders had faded faster than it should have. Agility showed in the cleaner recovery steps he had taken at the end of the battle. Mana sat deeper in his core, ready to answer the next call without the thin edge of strain he had felt earlier in the day. Even the single point of Intelligence mattered. The sealed attribute still frustrated him—he still did not know who or what had locked it—but any movement upward was proof that the lock was not absolute. If it could rise, it could eventually break.","He allocated nothing yet. The eight free points waited for a clearer sense of what the Wildlands would demand next.","The growth itself was the part that stayed with him as he started walking again. On Titan he had read the public reports and the quieter family records about average progression. Most new awakeners spent weeks clawing from level ten to fifteen. Parties of carefully balanced roles measured their gains in single levels per successful hunt, sometimes less. Cores were shared. Experience was divided. Here he had stepped outside the walls alone, killed one Savage-tier spider, and moved a full level while adding a meaningful stack of souls. The Abyssal Sovereign bloodline doubled everything that could be doubled. Analyze let him keep and slowly understand a second high-rank class. Infernomancer already gave him pressure, control, and finishing power that many pure fire mages took months to stabilize. Frostheart, even at a reduced rank and low mastery, had already saved his life and opened a second axis of attack.","By every ordinary measure the combination was unfair.","He still felt behind.","The feeling was not rational, and he knew it. His family had declared him classless in front of the entire capital. They had stripped his name and sent him away with a payment that was closer to a dismissal than a severance. In their eyes he remained the failed son, the one who had proven the bloodline could produce waste. Every level he gained, every skill that answered when he called, was still being measured against that public judgment. He wanted them to see the numbers. He wanted the elders who had nodded along with the expulsion to understand they had discarded the wrong person. The desire was blunt and more than a little childish—he could admit that without effort. Wanting acknowledgment after being erased was not a sophisticated motive. It was simply human. He had not yet grown past it.","He looked up. Both suns sat near their peak. In Aetherion that meant half the long daylight cycle was already gone. One full day here equaled two on Titan; night would arrive in roughly twenty-four hours of this realm’s time. He could not afford to be caught deep in the Wildlands after dark. Higher-tier monsters moved more freely once the twin suns set, and most sane awakeners returned to the walls before that hour. He was not yet strong enough to treat the rule as optional.","His gear had held. The short sword showed no new notches worth worrying about. The Nightfall Armor had taken web and impact without serious damage; he scraped the last sticky residue off while walking. The problem was not equipment quality. It was coverage. Hunters worked the zones nearest the city so thoroughly that low- and mid-tier monsters were sparse. If he wanted consistent fights he would have to move farther out, and distance on foot was a tax he could not keep paying. A mount had just moved up the list of necessities maybe a tamed horse or an wolf as he can’t afford any flying type beast as they are dangerously expensive even for him or he can copy a beast tamer class which would make things much easier.","An hour of steady travel produced no new contacts. The forest remained quiet in the way that suggested recent passage of other hunters rather than true emptiness. Then he saw the claw marks.","They were cut deep into the bark of a mature tree, higher than a man could comfortably reach, the grooves still pale at the center. Fresh. He crouched, found the corresponding prints in the softer ground, and began to follow. The trail led him through a denser stand of trees where the light thinned again. After several hundred meters a low, continuous growl reached him—close enough to be distinct, far enough that he was not yet in its line of sight.","Rael slowed, adjusted his path to keep cover between himself and the source of the sound, and moved forward with the careful steps of someone who had already learned that the Wildlands rewarded patience more than noise. The next fight was close. He intended to meet it on his feet and on his terms.","---","A/N:","(If you are reading this Chapter first of all I’m already thankful for purchasing premium Chapters and second to keep up with my sloppy work. I know the start of the novel was very fast forward and reached its conclusion very fast in future if I get time maybe I will fix those Chapters and lastly thank you for your support. Keep supporting by giving Golden Tickets and Power Stones. )"]},{"number":68,"title":"Chapter 68: Fang Wolf [1]","paragraphs":["Rael left the blackened clearing of the spider fight behind and pushed deeper into denser forest. The trees here grew closer together, their trunks thick and moss-dark, the canopy overhead knitting into a near-continuous roof that broke the twin sunlight into scattered coins on the ground. The air smelled of damp bark and old leaf litter. After the sharp chemical burn of venom and the lingering heat of his own fire, the cooler quiet felt almost clean.","He had been walking for some time when the low growl reached him.","It was not close enough to be an immediate threat, yet not far enough to ignore. Rael slowed, angled off the faint game trail he had been following, and moved toward the sound with deliberate care. Each footfall tested the ground before committing weight. He kept trunks between himself and the source, using the forest the way the spider had earlier used its webs—cover first, vision second.","Through a gap in the undergrowth he saw it.","A Fang Wolf stood over the torn remains of some smaller forest animal, muzzle buried in raw meat. Dark fur, heavier through the shoulders than an ordinary wolf, jaws built around a pair of oversized fangs that curved just enough to catch light. Blood darkened the ground beneath its front paws.","[Monster Detected]","Species: Fang Wolf","Level: 15","Tier: Savage","\"Argh! Bloody wolfs again.\" He has been through so much because of these wolfs in the first realm.","Rael held still and watched. The wolf ate with focused hunger, ears swiveling independently, but its attention remained on the meal. He shifted his weight to ease a root under his boot.","The branch snapped.","It was a small sound, almost modest, yet in the relative silence it might as well have been a shout. The Fang Wolf’s head snapped up. Its ears locked forward. Yellow eyes found him through the leaves and held. The growl that rolled out of its chest was lower and more continuous than the first—one predator acknowledging another that had stepped into its space. Hunger and fury mixed in the sound. Fresh meat was good; warmer meat that still moved was better.","It did not charge at once. The wolf took one slow lateral step, then another, studying him. Rael used the pause. He noted the exaggerated length of the fangs, the heavy muscle bunched in the hind legs, the way its weight sat ready to explode forward. This was an animal built for short, devastating bursts rather than long pursuit.","He made a quiet decision while they watched each other. No magic. Not for this fight. Swordsmanship practiced on polished floors and against human opponents was not the same as swordsmanship tested against something that wanted to open his throat. If he kept leaning on Inferno Bolt and Frostheart for every threat, the gap in his close combat would only widen. This single wolf was a chance to close that gap by force of repetition.","The Fang Wolf moved.","It closed the distance in a single explosive lunge, front claws reaching, jaws already open for a follow-up bite. Rael drew the Ironfang short sword in the same motion that brought his feet into the basic Blackthorn middle guard. Steel met keratin with a jarring clang. The impact traveled up his arm and drove him back three full steps; his boots tore shallow tracks in the leaf litter. The wolf’s raw power was greater than its level suggested.","It did not press the advantage immediately. Instead it began to circle, head low, eyes never leaving his blade. Rael turned with it, keeping the sword between them, weight centered the way the old tutors had demanded. He could feel the difference between drill and reality in every small adjustment—the way the wolf’s shoulders telegraphed less cleanly than a human’s, the way the forest floor gave underfoot at unpredictable moments.","He decided to invite the next attack.","Rael let his right side open a fraction, blade angle shifting just enough to look like a mistake. The wolf took the invitation without hesitation. It drove hard toward the exposed flank, fangs aimed to lock and tear. Rael’s pivot was small and late enough to sell the opening, then sharp. The wolf’s momentum carried it past the space he had occupied. As it went by he cut once, a short descending slash that opened a clean line across its ribs. Blood welled dark against the fur.","The animal recovered with frightening speed. It landed, twisted, and came again almost before the first wound had fully registered. Rael met the second rush with a parry that redirected rather than stopped the force, then a third, then a fourth. Each exchange taught him the same lesson more clearly: the wolf’s threat lived in the first explosive second. If he survived the burst and kept his footing, the following movements were slightly less precise, slightly easier to read.","He stopped chasing.","Instead of advancing when the wolf retreated a step, he held position and waited. The creature prowled the edge of the small clearing they had trampled, looking for a new angle. Rael kept the sword in a high guard and forced his breathing to stay even. Patience was part of the form; the tutors had repeated it until the words lost meaning. Out here the meaning returned.","The Fang Wolf committed.","It launched in a straight, full-power line, everything committed to overwhelming him before he could react. Rael’s pivot this time was earlier and cleaner. He stepped off-line at the last moment, let the bulk of the animal pass under his shoulder, and brought the short sword across in a single decisive cut aimed at the neck and upper shoulder. The blade bit deep. The wolf’s forward drive turned into a collapsing slide. It hit the ground hard, tried to rise once, and failed. The yellow eyes stayed open for a few seconds longer, then emptied.","[+15 Souls.]","Rael stood over the body, sword still raised, until he was certain no reflex remained. Blood dripped from the tip of the Ironfang and spotted the leaves. His own breathing was heavier than he liked, but the cuts he had taken were shallow—one across the forearm where a claw had grazed him during the second exchange, another scrape along the ribs where the armor had done its job.","He looked down at the still form.","\"Fast, but predictable.\"","The system remained quiet on the matter of levels. No surge of experience, no notification of growth. The wolf had been only a single level above him and the fight, while sharp, had not demanded the same intensity as the spider. He would need harder opponents—and more of them—if he wanted the numbers to keep moving.","---"]},{"number":69,"title":"Chapter 69: Fang Wolf [2]","paragraphs":["Rael crouched beside the Fang Wolf and forced his hand into the chest cavity until his fingers closed around the core. It came free with a wet pull, smaller than the spider’s and duller in color, but still usable. He stored it without lingering.","His gaze shifted to the place where the wolf had been feeding.","The trees around the kill site were marked. Not with one set of claws. With many. Pale grooves crossed darker, older scars. Some sat at shoulder height. Others reached higher, as if several animals had risen onto hind legs to claim the same trunks. Too many lines. Too many directions. The single wolf he had just killed could not have made them all.","Rael straightened. The short sword stayed in his hand.","A howl rose from somewhere to the east—long, low, and answered almost at once from the north. A third voice joined from farther back in the trees. The sounds were not random. They were locating each other. They were locating him.","Yellow eyes opened in the undergrowth.","They appeared in pairs, then in clusters, catching what little light reached the forest floor. Shapes followed the eyes: dark fur, heavy shoulders, the same oversized fangs he had already seen up close. The pack did not rush. They stepped into view one by one, closing a rough circle around the small clearing. Rael counted as they emerged. Ten. Eleven. Twelve, if he included the ones still half-hidden behind trunks.","He tightened his grip on the Ironfang.","A thin smile touched his mouth.","\"So that’s why you weren’t afraid.\"","The dead wolf had fought like an animal that expected help. Now the help had arrived. The pack’s attention moved from the corpse at his feet to the human standing over it. Anger ran through them in a visible current—hackles lifting, lips peeling back from those long fangs, a low chorus of growls that overlapped until it became a single vibrating note. They wanted blood for the one already cooling in the leaves.","They still did not charge as one.","Instead they began to move. The circle rotated. Wolves changed places without breaking the ring, some drifting closer, others slipping behind trunks and reappearing at a new angle. Rael turned with them, sword tracking the nearest threat, then the next. They were testing the speed of his head, the width of his guard, the moment his weight shifted from one foot to the other. A pack that hunted together learned the prey before it committed.","The first attack came from the front.","A wolf exploded out of the ring in the same bursting lunge he had already survived once. Rael met it with a short, hard block. Steel caught claws. The impact shoved him back a step. He had no time to reset.","The second came from behind.","He heard the leaves tear an instant before the body arrived. He spun, bringing the blade around in a tight arc that was more deflection than cut. Claws raked past his shoulder close enough to scrape the Nightfall Armor. He felt the shock through the plates and kept turning, because the third was already coming from the left.","That one he met earlier. The sword cut across its muzzle and opened a deep line that sent it yelping sideways. Blood hit the ground. The opening lasted less than a breath.","The rest of the pack closed the distance at once.","They did not wait for him to finish the wounded animal. Two more rushed the space he had used to strike. Rael gave ground because standing still meant being taken from three directions at once. His boots slid over roots and torn earth. A jaw snapped shut where his calf had been. Another body slammed into his guard and drove him another two steps toward the trees at his back.","He understood the problem with ugly clarity.","Killing one wolf did not end the fight. The pack treated losses as fuel. Every animal he dropped only tightened the remaining ring.","\"Dammit,\" he muttered. \"Wolves don’t travel alone.\"","There was always another.","He changed the way he used the sword.","Wide, committed cuts were a luxury he no longer had. Each full swing left a window, and the pack had already shown they knew how to use windows. He shortened everything. The Blackthorn forms compressed into their smallest versions—half-steps, tight parries, cuts that traveled no farther than necessary to open hide or break a committing lunge. He stopped trying to kill with every motion and started trying to survive the next three seconds.","A wolf came in low. He met it with a descending chop that split the skull behind the eyes. The body dropped. Before it finished falling, another took its place from the right, fangs aimed at his sword arm. He turned the blade, let the animal’s own momentum carry it onto the edge, and shoved the corpse aside with his shoulder. Blood ran down the fuller and made the grip slick. He adjusted his fingers and kept moving.","The pack adapted as quickly as he did.","They began staggering their bursts. One feinted high while another drove at his legs. A third waited just outside the range of his turn, ready to take the opening created by the first two. Rael’s world narrowed to angles and timing. The forest beyond the circle ceased to exist. There was only fur, teeth, the wet sound of impact, and the growing burn in his shoulders.","He killed another.","It did not matter. The ring closed over the space the dead wolf left behind as if the animal had never been there. Twelve had become fewer, but not few enough. The remaining wolves pressed harder, anger no longer mixed with caution. They had paid for information. Now they intended to spend it.","Rael’s expression settled into something flatter and more serious than the thin smile he had worn at the start.","Fighting one had been a lesson.","Fighting all of them was a different language.","He was still speaking it with a sword, still refusing to reach for fire or ice, still forcing the gap in his close combat to close under pressure that did not care whether he learned or died. The promise he had made to himself after Lila—not to lose what was his—felt very close and very thin. Right now what was his was the space inside the circle, the blade in his hand, and the next breath.","A wolf broke from the left. Another from the front. A third gathered itself behind a trunk for a leaping strike at his back. The pack had stopped testing. They had started the real hunt.","Rael set his feet, raised the short sword into the tightest guard he had, and waited for the next body to arrive.","---"]},{"number":70,"title":"Chapter 70: Fang Wolf [3]","paragraphs":["The pack changed the shape of the hunt.","Two wolves drove straight at his front in a paired rush, spaced just far enough apart that a single sweep of the sword could not catch both. At the same time three others slipped behind him, not charging yet, only tightening the space at his back so that any retreat would put him into teeth. The remaining animals held the outer ring and waited for the first mistakes.","Rael met the lead wolf with a hard block. Steel rang against claws. The second animal came in a half-beat later, aiming for the opening the first impact created. He dropped his weight and let that one pass over his shoulder, feeling fur brush the side of his neck. Before he could fully rise, a third head snapped toward his calf. He drove his boot into the wolf’s muzzle hard enough to send it tumbling, jaws clicking shut on empty air.","He never saw the one in the tree until it was already falling.","It launched from a low branch with all four legs gathered, a dark mass dropping out of the canopy. Rael got the short sword up in time to put steel between himself and the animal’s chest. The collision still took him off his feet. The impact lifted him and threw him several meters backward. He hit the ground hard, rolled, and came up on one knee with the breath knocked half out of him.","He did not get the chance to stand cleanly.","Two more wolves were already committed to the space he had just vacated, closing while his balance was still broken. The stubborn plan of winning this with the sword alone lasted exactly as long as it took him to see that next pair of bodies in the air. Pride would not keep his throat closed.","Flame Manipulation.","Fire tore up from the ground in a short, violent wall between him and the incoming pair. The heat hit them in the face. Instinct overrode coordination. Both wolves broke sideways with sharp, startled yelps, claws tearing dirt as they scrambled out of the burn. The rest of the pack flinched as one, the ring loosening for the first time since it had closed.","Rael used the gap. He came fully to his feet, put a trunk at his left shoulder, and reset his stance where the fire gave him a temporary flank. His lungs still burned from the throw. His sword arm ached. The pack was already gathering itself again, yellow eyes reflecting the dying wall of flame.","Watching them reform taught him the thing he should have named sooner.","They were not attacking at random.","Every lunge had been timed against another. Every feint had existed to create a second angle. When one wolf died, the others did not hesitate in grief; they stepped into the empty place as if a single mind had simply withdrawn a limb and extended another. Twelve bodies. One hunter.","\"They’re not attacking randomly,\" he said under his breath.","If he kept answering each animal as a separate problem, they would spend him down to bone. He needed to break the shape, not the individuals.","Hellfire.","He put the skill into the ground along one entire side of the clearing—a long, stubborn band of dark-red fire that clung to soil and root and refused to go out. The wolves on that side recoiled as the heat climbed their legs. The ring tore open. Three animals were forced wide, cut off from the main body by a line of flame they would not cross. For a few seconds the pack was no longer one creature. It was a cluster and a remnant.","Rael went at the remnant immediately.","The nearest isolated wolf met him with a rising lunge. He stepped inside it and put the short sword through the base of the neck in a single short thrust. The body dropped. Movement flashed at his back. He turned on the ball of his foot and cut across the second animal’s shoulder as it committed, opening a deep wound that collapsed the leg on that side. The wolf screamed and tried to twist away. He did not chase it.","The third came in lower, aiming to take him at the hip while his blade was still occupied. Rael’s free hand came up.","Frost Bolt.","Ice formed and flew in the same motion. The spear took the wolf through the front left leg and detonated into a spreading sheath of white. The limb locked. The animal’s charge became a crash. It hit the ground hard, skidding, unable to rise cleanly on three legs. Rael closed the last two steps and finished it with a downward cut that ended the sound.","Three down on the broken side. The main body of the pack had not wasted the time.","They were already flowing around the Hellfire, finding the ends of the burning line, reconstituting the circle with the animals that remained. Fewer now, but not confused. The coordination had not died with the isolated wolves. It had simply redrawn itself around the new numbers. They had watched him use fire to split them. They would not offer him the same clean separation a second time.","Rael’s attention was pulled in too many directions at once.","A wolf came from the front. He blocked. Steel shivered in his grip. Another animal raked his left shoulder before he could complete the turn; claws skipped off the Nightfall Armor and still found enough flesh to open a burning line. He hissed, pivoted, and saw the third too late—already in the air, jaws open for his throat. He threw himself aside. Fangs closed on the space his neck had occupied and tore a strip from the edge of his collar instead of his windpipe.","He hit one knee, came up again, and understood the new rule with perfect clarity.","He could not afford a single second of divided focus. Not to check a wound. Not to watch a body fall. Not to admire a skill landing cleanly. The pack had adapted to the fire. They would adapt to the ice. They would keep spending bodies until his concentration slipped once, and once would be enough.","The remaining wolves tightened the ring again, slower now, more careful, yellow eyes bright in the shifting light of the still-burning Hellfire. Rael raised the blood-slick sword, felt the heat and the cold both waiting under his skin, and held the center of a fight that no longer had any simple edges.","---"]},{"number":71,"title":"Chapter 71: Fang Wolf [4]","paragraphs":["Chapter 71","More than half the pack already lay dead or dying in the torn clearing. The survivors no longer threw themselves forward in the same coordinated mass. The losses had taught them. They changed the rhythm of the hunt.","Instead of pressing from every direction at once, they began to work in alternating waves. Two would drive in hard while the others held back, waiting for the moment his blade or attention committed. The moment he answered the first pair, another pair moved. The pressure never fully lifted. Rael was forced to keep moving, boots scraping over roots and blood-slick leaves, never allowed a clean second to reset his stance.","The Ironfang short sword felt heavier than it had at the start. Not by much—just enough that each parry arrived a fraction later than he wanted. His breathing had deepened. The long exchange with the spider, then the single wolf, then this running battle against the pack was stacking fatigue in his shoulders and lungs. He could still fight. He could not fight forever like this.","\"Keep the feet moving,\" he muttered under his breath. \"Don’t plant. Don’t give them a still target.\"","A wolf broke from the left in a low, fast rush. Rael met the claws with a short, economical block and shoved the animal aside. Before the first had fully cleared, another came from the opposite angle, jaws open for his thigh. He twisted, felt teeth scrape across the outer layer of his trousers, and tore free with a sharp kick. Fabric ripped. Cold air touched skin where the cloth had given way.","He did not wait for the next set of fangs to find the same place.","Ice Armor.","Frost slammed across his torso, shoulders, and upper arms in a hard, translucent shell. The next wolf hit the ice with a solid, bone-jarring impact and bounced off with a startled yelp, claws scrabbling for purchase that was no longer there. The armor cracked in a thin spiderweb pattern but held. Rael used the rebound to step back into clearer ground.","\"Not enough,\" he told himself. \"They’re still too many. Break the ground under them.\"","Frost Manipulation.","He dragged the cold downward. Moisture in the soil flash-froze into a wide, uneven sheet of ice that spread under the nearest cluster of wolves. Paws shot out from under them. Bodies slammed into the frozen earth in a tangle of fur and thrashing legs. For a few seconds the alternating rhythm shattered.","Rael moved into the opening without hesitation.","He did not waste motion on wide, dramatic cuts. The sword work stayed short and final—thrust to the throat of the first animal still trying to rise, descending cut across the spine of the second, a pivoting slash that opened the third from shoulder to ribs as it scrambled for traction. Each body dropped. The remaining wolves scrambled back from the ice, fury and sudden caution warring in their postures.","When the last of the slipped animals either died or regained its feet outside the frozen patch, only three Fang Wolves remained.","Rael reset his grip on the blood-slick hilt. The Ice Armor was already fracturing and falling away in dull white pieces. His breathing was harder now, but the numbers had finally tipped.","\"Three,\" he said quietly. \"Finish it.\"","He started forward.","A howl stopped him.","It was not like the earlier calls that had gathered the pack. This one was deeper, heavier, and carried a pressure that seemed to push against the chest. The sound rolled through the trees and made the leaves tremble. The three remaining wolves froze mid-step. Their heads dropped. Their bodies lowered. The aggressive line of their shoulders collapsed into something closer to submission.","Rael’s eyes moved past them into the darker spaces between the trunks.","\"That isn’t one of them.\"","Something large was coming.","The undergrowth parted. A wolf stepped into the thinning light of the clearing, and the difference was immediate. It stood significantly larger than any of the Fang Wolves he had already killed—shoulders higher, chest broader, legs thicker with muscle. Its fur was a deeper, almost black shade that seemed to drink the light. The fangs that curved from its upper jaw were longer and thicker than the others, built for crushing as much as tearing. Yellow eyes fixed on him with a steady, intelligent focus that the rest of the pack had never quite matched.","The three surviving wolves retreated behind it without needing an order, heads still low.","Rael understood at once.","[Monster Detected]","Species: Fang Alpha","Level: 24","Tier: Savage","The Alpha did not rush. It began to circle him at a measured pace, paws silent on the torn ground, eyes never leaving his. There was no mindless hunger in the movement. It was evaluating—distance, stance, the way he held the sword, the residual frost still melting from his armor, the blood already on his clothes. Rael turned with it, keeping the blade between them, feeling the weight of being examined by something that had clearly survived longer than any of its packmates.","\"You’re thinking,\" he murmured. \"That’s new.\"","The Alpha vanished.","One moment it was pacing along the edge of his peripheral vision. The next it was simply gone—no blur, no leap he could track, just absence. Rael’s body reacted before his mind fully caught up. He brought the short sword up across his body in a tight, rising guard.","CLANG!","Claws smashed into the blade with a force that numbed his entire arm. The impact drove him backward across the clearing in a sliding, uncontrolled skid. Boots tore long furrows through dirt and leaves. He barely kept his feet. Before he could fully stop the slide or reset his weight, the Alpha was already on him again.","He blocked the second strike. Steel rang. Sparks jumped. The Alpha’s weight behind the blow was greater than any of the ordinary wolves had managed. Rael’s guard held, but only just.","Then the Alpha changed direction in the middle of the motion—an impossible-looking mid-attack adjustment that took it off the line of his sword and brought the other set of claws across his side. The Nightfall Armor caught most of it. Enough force still transferred to tear a burning line along his ribs and spin him half around. Rael threw himself backward in a short, ugly jump to create space, landing hard and bringing the sword back up.","For the first time since the pack had closed around him, his expression lost every trace of calculation or thin confidence. The look that settled on his face was completely serious.","\"You’re much faster than the others.\"","The Alpha stood a short distance away, unhurried, the three remaining pack wolves still crouched behind it. It was not breathing hard. It had not yet committed everything. The single exchange had already told Rael the gap between a common Fang Wolf and this one was not a matter of size alone. Speed, power, and the ability to read and adjust in the middle of a strike—those were the differences that got people killed when they treated an Alpha like just another animal.","Rael adjusted his grip, ignored the fresh pain along his side, and settled into a lower stance. The forest around them had gone very quiet. The remaining Hellfire from earlier had burned down to scattered embers. The ice was melting. All that was left was the Alpha, the three watching wolves, and the clear understanding that the real fight had only just arrived.","He exhaled once, steadying the next breath.","\"All right,\" he said to himself, voice low. \"Show me what you’ve got.\"","The Fang Alpha’s eyes narrowed. Its weight shifted forward by a fraction.","The next move would not be a test.","---"]},{"number":72,"title":"Chapter 72: Fang Wolf [5]","paragraphs":["Chapter 72","Alpha of the Fang","The Fang Alpha moved first.","It came in a hard, low line, faster than the earlier exchange had prepared him for. Rael met the rush the only way that still made sense—he pulled fire into the short sword.","Flame Manipulation.","Heat raced along the steel until the blade burned with a tight, white-edged sheath of flame. The Alpha saw the change and threw itself sideways at the last instant. The flaming edge carved a bright arc through empty air and left a trail of heat that made the nearby leaves curl.","Rael did not let the miss end the sequence. He stepped through and brought a second cut up from the low line, aiming for the shoulder. The Alpha twisted and caught the blade on its claws. Fire and keratin met with a sharp, spitting hiss. The impact shuddered up Rael’s arms. The Alpha’s strength was still greater. Raw power alone would not break it.","They traded again. Claws against flaming steel. Short, violent collisions that forced Rael back a step each time. The Alpha’s weight and the density of its strikes kept stealing the center of the clearing from him.","\"I can’t match it like this,\" he said under his breath, voice tight with the effort of another parry. \"Strength isn’t the road. Control the ground.\"","He stopped answering every attack with a direct cut.","Hellfire.","He put the skill down along the entire left side of the clearing. Dark-red fire climbed out of the soil in a continuous, stubborn band that clung to root and leaf and refused to die. The Alpha’s next lateral move brought it close enough to feel the heat. It checked hard and angled away from the flames.","Rael was already working the opposite side.","Frost Manipulation.","Cold pulled moisture from the air and the earth. A spreading sheet of ice raced across the right flank of the clearing, locking the ground into a slick, uneven surface. In the space of a few heartbeats the Alpha’s clean escape routes narrowed. Fire on one side. Ice on the other. The open path that remained led straight toward the human standing in the middle with a burning sword.","The Alpha took it.","It committed to a full-power charge, everything poured into closing the distance before more of the ground could be taken from it. Rael held position and watched the timing with the same cold focus he had used against the spider. He waited until the massive body filled his vision and the claws were already descending.","Ice Armor.","The shell of hardened frost locked across his torso and arms in the final fraction of a second. The Alpha hit him like a falling tree. The impact drove the air from his lungs and threw him backward across the clearing. He hit, slid, and rolled, the armor cracking and flaking under the force but keeping the claws from opening his chest. When he came up his ribs ached and his vision had narrowed, but he was still breathing.","The Alpha was already turning for the follow-up.","Rael raised his free hand before it could complete the pivot.","\"Inferno Bolt.\"","The compressed spear of white-hot fire formed and flew. It struck the Alpha high on the side and detonated. The explosion tore a ragged, burning wound through fur and muscle. The Alpha staggered, one leg dipping, a rough snarl ripping out of its throat. Smoke rose from the injury. It still did not go down. It planted its feet, gathered whatever strength remained, and prepared one final charge.","Rael tightened his grip on the short sword.","\"One more,\" he told himself. \"Make it count.\"","Flame Manipulation answered again, denser this time. Fire concentrated along the blade until the steel itself seemed secondary to the cutting heat that wrapped it. The Alpha launched. The leap was still fast, still heavy with killing intent, but the earlier wound and the damaged ground had stolen some of its cleanness.","Rael did not retreat.","He stepped forward into the attack, dropped his weight, and slipped under the reaching claws. Fur and heat passed over his back. As the Alpha’s momentum carried it past, Rael turned and drove an upward slash with everything he had left. The flaming edge bit deep, continued, and tore a long, catastrophic line through the chest and upper torso.","The Fang Alpha’s forward drive died in mid-air. The body crashed to the ground in a heavy, final sprawl. Legs kicked once. The yellow eyes stayed open for a moment longer, then emptied.","Silence returned to the forest by degrees.","The last thin crackle of Hellfire faded. The ice had already begun to melt. The three remaining ordinary wolves that had watched from the edge were gone—fled the moment their leader fell. Rael stood over the Alpha’s body with the short sword still raised, chest rising and falling in deep, controlled pulls. Blood from earlier cuts had dried in dark lines across his armor. New bruises were already settling along his ribs and shoulder. Nothing was broken. Nothing was bleeding badly enough to force a potion yet. He had walked into the fight with a pack and walked out of it with only minor injuries. The margin had been thinner than he liked.","The system registered the kill.","[+1 Level up]","[+159 Souls]","Rael exhaled and finally lowered the blade. He looked across the clearing at the scattered corpses of the pack and then back to the massive body at his feet.","\"The difference between a beast and its leader,\" he said quietly, \"isn’t always strength.\"","He turned the thought over once more.","\"Sometimes it’s understanding.\"","The Alpha had read him, adapted mid-strike, and forced him to abandon pure power for control of the ground. That was the gap. Size and speed mattered. The mind behind them mattered more.","He crouched, forced his hand into the ruined chest, and pulled the Monster Core free. It was denser and brighter than the ordinary Fang Wolf cores, warm even through the residual heat of the fight. He stored it, then stored what he could of the useful remains. His arms felt leaden. The fresh level sat in his body like a quiet second wind, but it was not enough to make another serious fight wise.","He looked once more at the darkening spaces between the trees. The twin suns had moved further along their path. Time had been spent. Condition had been spent. Staying out longer would be greed, not growth.","Rael turned back toward the distant direction of the walls, adjusted the fit of his armor where the Alpha’s claws had scraped it, and began the walk out of the Wildlands. The forest watched him go in silence.","---"]},{"number":73,"title":"Chapter 73: First night","paragraphs":["The light of the twin suns had begun to soften by the time Rael turned back toward the walls. The long daylight of Aetherion was tipping toward its end, and the first cooler currents of approaching night moved through the trees. At his level, remaining outside after dark was close to a death sentence. Higher-tier monsters stirred once the suns dropped, and the Wildlands stopped pretending to be merely dangerous. He kept a steady pace, checking the line of the massive wall whenever the canopy thinned. Soon the dark stone bulk filled the horizon. No one with working eyes could miss something that large.","Closer in, the outbound and inbound traffic thickened. Hunters and small parties streamed toward the gate in uneven clusters. Some walked with the loose, joking energy of a clean day—shoulders relaxed, voices carrying, the occasional laugh cutting through the general murmur. Others moved in exhausted silence, armor scored, faces gray with fatigue. A few limped. One man was being carried between two companions, head hanging. Success and near-failure mixed on the same road without ceremony.","Rael recognized one group before he reached them. Kira’s party. There were only three now. The archer leaned heavily on Kira, the sleeve on one side of his body empty and hastily bound. The spear-girl walked a step behind, expression tight. They had left with five. They were returning with three and a permanent injury. High-level regeneration or a specialist healer might eventually restore the limb, but that kind of help sat far above the reach of most new awakeners. The archer’s career as a bow user was effectively over unless someone paid a fortune.","Kira’s eyes lifted and met Rael’s. He took in the fact that the lone traveler looked tired but intact—no missing pieces, no desperate lean on a companion. Frustration flickered across Kira’s face. He clicked his tongue once, adjusted his grip on the archer, and turned toward the gate without a word. Rael felt a brief, distant pity and then let it go. The thought rose unbidden—what if I had gone with them?—and he shut it down the same way he had shut down similar thoughts since the First Realm. Party or no party, the Wildlands did not hand out fairness. He might have lost an arm himself. They might have run and left him. After Aurelia, trust was no longer something he spent lightly.","The gate plaza opened around him in a broad, stone-paved space still busy with the last of the day’s traffic. Royal knights in white plate kept the lines moving. Conversations overlapped in a constant low roar. Rael slowed enough to let the voices wash past while he waited his turn.","\"...core quality was garbage, but the volume made up for it—\"","\"I’m telling you the eastern trail is picked clean. We need to push past the second ridge or stop wasting time.\"","\"Healer prices went up again. Bandage and minor potion almost isn’t worth the coin if you’re only taking scratches.\"","\"Next time we bring a proper tank. Glass-cannon parties are done after today.\"","\"Did you see the size of that horned thing near the old watchtower? Level twenty at least. We backed off.\"","\"Night’s coming. Anyone still out past full dark is asking for it.\"","Pointers, complaints, quiet boasts, and hard-earned corrections mixed together. The gate was where the day’s lessons were traded before people scattered to inns, guild halls, or the road back to Titan. Rael listened without joining. Information cost nothing if you kept your ears open.","The bald knight from the morning was still on duty. When Rael stepped up to the checkpoint the man glanced at his pass, then at his face, and the stern line of his mouth eased into something closer to relief.","\"Made it back, lone wolf.\"","Rael nodded once. He had never held a grudge for the earlier bluntness. The advice had been genuine, even if the delivery was rough. The knight waved him through. Beyond the thick tunnel of the gate the city proper began again.","Ashfall at the edge of night felt different from Ashfall under the full twin suns. Mana-stone street lamps were flickering to life along the main avenues—soft, steady glows that pushed the gathering blue hour back from the paving stones. The light was cleaner than Titan’s older electric systems and carried a faint, pleasant warmth. Shops that had been open to the street began closing shutters or switching to evening lanterns. The smell of cooking drifted from side alleys and open doorways: grilling meat, baking bread, the sharper note of stewed herbs. People moved with the particular energy of a city shifting from work to rest—some still armored and dusty from the Wildlands, others already changed into lighter clothes, voices rising as the day’s tension loosened.","Rael had no intention of returning to Titan tonight. The apartment would still be there. Aetherion was the place he needed to learn, and learning required time spent inside its walls as well as outside them. An inn would do. He could sell the cores and usable remains tomorrow at the Adventurer Guild, or he could absorb some of the mana stones later if he decided pure capacity mattered more than coin in the short term. First priority was a door that locked and a place to sit that was not forest floor.","He stopped a young woman in a wide-brimmed mage’s hat who was walking with two friends.","\"Excuse me. Can you tell me where I might find an inn? Something ordinary, not the expensive ones.\"","She startled, then actually looked at him. Rael was no longer running Conceal. Black hair, pale skin, the residual sharpness that hard fighting left on a face—whatever she saw made her pause long enough for her friends to notice and drift closer, openly listening. The mage girl recovered with visible effort and answered, voice catching once or twice on the directions. When he thanked her and started to turn away she found another fraction of courage.","\"Wait—would you maybe want to go on a hunt sometime? Together?\"","Rael considered it for half a second. The city was large enough that the odds of meeting them again by chance were low, but refusing every human contact out of principle was its own kind of stupidity. He might need a party later. Burning bridges for no reason gained him nothing.","\"All right. Another time.\"","He left them in a small storm of excited whispers and walked the route she had given. The streets grew narrower and warmer with lamp light. The smell of food strengthened until it was impossible to ignore. He followed it to a well-kept inn whose front windows spilled gold onto the street. The sign was simple, the door open, the sound of conversation and cutlery drifting out.","Inside, the common room was already half full. Wooden tables, low-beamed ceiling, mana lamps set in iron brackets along the walls. The air was rich with roasting meat, fresh bread, and something sweet baking in the back. Servers moved between the tables with practiced efficiency. Most of them were ordinary humans. One was not.","She wore a fitted maid uniform that had clearly been altered for effect—shortened skirt, neat apron, the usual details—and a pair of soft, expressive bunny ears that flicked occasionally as she worked. Mixed blood. Rael had known other races existed in Aetherion; this was the first time he had seen one up close. She looked a few years older than him, pretty in a lively, approachable way, and the customers tracked her movement with open appreciation. More than one table seemed to have chosen the inn specifically because she was on shift.","Rael took a side table and waited. She reached him within a minute, order book already in hand, smile ready.","\"Hello there, handsome. First time under this roof?\"","\"Yes.\"","\"I’m Mira. What can I get for you tonight?\"","\"Menu, please. And I’d like to know if you have any rooms left.\"","Mira’s ears gave a small, amused twitch. She leaned a fraction closer, voice dropping into something playful and deliberately suggestive.","\"Well now. My own room happens to be empty. Should I show you the way, or are you only looking for the boring kind of bed?\"","Rael blinked once. \"I meant a room at the inn.\"","She laughed, light and unoffended, and straightened.","\"I know, I know. A girl’s allowed a little fun when someone new and pretty walks in looking half-dead from the Wildlands. Try smiling more—it suits you. Yes, we have rooms. I’ll take you up after you’ve eaten so you don’t fall over on the stairs. Here’s the menu. Take your time. I’ll be back before you finish deciding.\"","She set the card down, winked once, and moved off to another table with the same easy sway that explained why the common room stayed full. Rael watched her go for a moment, then looked down at the menu without really seeing it yet. The noise of the inn settled around him—clink of plates, low talk, the occasional burst of laughter, the warm smell of food that had nothing to do with scorched monster meat or trail rations. Outside, full night was arriving. Inside, for the first time since he had stepped through the gate that morning, the world felt briefly simple.","He was still in Aetherion. He was still alive. Tomorrow he would sell what he had taken, check his growth, and decide how much farther into the Wildlands he was ready to push. Tonight he only needed a meal and a locked door.","Mira’s voice rose again somewhere across the room, teasing another customer with the same bright, practiced charm. Rael finally began to read the menu, the mana-lamp light steady on the page, and let the ordinary noise of the inn push the last of the forest silence out of his head.","---"]},{"number":74,"title":"Chapter 74: Mira","paragraphs":["Rael opened the menu and scanned the short list of evening dishes. Most of it was straightforward—grilled cuts, stews, bread, a few sides he did not recognize. He settled on the beef stew. When Mira returned he placed the order without looking up for long.","\"Beef stew.\"","\"Good choice,\" she said, already writing it down. \"It’ll be out soon, handsome.\"","She left with a small sway that was probably deliberate. Rael’s eyes followed the motion of her hips before he caught himself and looked hard at the tabletop. Heat crawled up the back of his neck. He did not understand the sudden interest. He had never spent time staring at women like that before. The thought arrived uncomfortably clear: was this puberty finally arriving in full? The timing felt ridiculous. He had fought for his life in the First Realm, walked out of a family that discarded him, and only now his body decided to notice curves and short skirts. First Elena next door—calm, mature, older—and now this silver-haired rabbit-folk woman who looked years past him and spoke like she knew exactly what she was doing. He exhaled through his nose and forced the subject closed. Feeling it was one thing. Acting on it here was another.","Mira came back with the bowl and set it down closer than necessary. She leaned in to adjust the spoon and the edge of a napkin, which put the front of her fitted uniform uncomfortably near his face. The soft press of fabric and the light scent of whatever soap she used hit him at the same time. Heat that had nothing to do with the stew pooled lower. He kept his gaze on the bowl and his hands flat on the table.","\"There you go,\" she said, voice warm and amused. \"Careful, it’s hot. Wouldn’t want you burning that pretty mouth.\"","\"Thank you.\"","She lingered half a second longer than the job required, then straightened with a small, knowing smile and moved to another table. Rael waited until she was gone before he picked up the spoon. The stew was better than he expected—rich, properly seasoned, the meat tender enough that it did not fight the bite. He had assumed most of the customers came only to watch Mira. The food itself was a legitimate reason to return. He ate steadily, using the simple act to push the earlier awareness back down where it belonged.","When the bowl was empty he sat back and waited. Mira noticed and returned, wiping her hands on her apron.","\"So, handsome—how was the stew?\"","Rael felt the tips of his ears warm again. \"It’s good. Actually very good. And... I’d prefer if you didn’t keep calling me that. It’s embarrassing.\"","Mira’s ears gave a delighted little flick. \"Come on. You’re no fun. Fine. What should I call you instead, sweetheart?\"","\"Rael.\"","\"Rael.\" She tested the name like she was tasting it. \"All right, Rael. I’m Mira, in case you forgot. Silver hair, these ears, the one who keeps making you turn red.\" She tilted her head, studying him with open playfulness. \"You’re trying so hard to look serious. It’s cute.\"","He understood then, with slightly more clarity, that the teasing was not real interest. She was older, experienced, and clearly entertained by how easily he reacted. To her he was probably just a young customer who had only recently discovered certain feelings and had not yet learned to hide them. She was poking the reaction for her own amusement, nothing more. That knowledge helped. A little.","\"How much for a room?\" he asked, keeping his voice even.","Mira’s expression shifted into something more professional, though the spark in her eyes stayed. \"We have a few types. Come on, I’ll show you so you can decide. Better than me talking at you down here.\"","She turned and led the way toward a side stair. Rael followed. The skirt of her uniform was short enough that the climb made looking anywhere else a deliberate effort. He fixed his eyes on the railing and the wall and told himself, not for the first time that evening, that short skirts should be illegal around people who were still getting used to their own pulse. His ears felt hot again. He said nothing.","On the second floor she stopped at a door on the right, unlocked it, and pushed it open.","\"You’re alone, so you won’t need a double. And you don’t look like someone throwing platinum around. This should do.\"","The room was modest—single bed, small table, a window that looked out over a quieter side street, clean but nothing luxurious. It was smaller than his apartment on Titan and felt temporary in the way inn rooms always did. It was also exactly what he needed.","\"This is fine,\" he said. \"How much?\"","\"We charge by the day, the week, or longer. Longer stays get a discount. This room is ten silver a night. Take it for a week and it’s sixty. You save ten.\"","Rael did the quiet arithmetic. Sixty silver was not trivial, but it was manageable. The food was good, the location was inside the walls, and he could stay in Aetherion without burning through his coins too quickly. The only ongoing complication was the woman currently standing in the doorway watching him with that same teasing patience.","Mira stepped closer, one hand lifting to rest lightly against the side of her own chest in a gesture that was pure performance. \"What do you think, Rael? Sweet enough deal for you?\"","The movement pulled his eyes for a fraction of a second before he locked them back on her face. Embarrassment flared, sharp and immediate. He held onto his patience the way he had held onto the sword against the beasts he fought—tight, deliberate, refusing to give the reaction she was fishing for more than the minimum.","\"The price is fine,\" he said. \"I’ll pay for the week. On one condition.\"","\"Oh?\"","\"Don’t stand so close to me. And ease up on the... rest of it.\"","Mira put a hand over her heart as if wounded, then laughed, soft and unoffended. \"There it is. The serious face again. All right, all right. I’ll behave. Mostly.\" She held out a hand. \"Sixty silver, and the room’s yours until the same day next week.\"","Rael counted the coins and placed them in her palm. Her fingers closed over them, warm and brief. She stepped back at last, giving him the space he had asked for.","\"Enjoy the bed, Rael. If you need extra blankets or a late meal, just come down and ask. I’ll try not to make you blush too hard.\" Another small wink, gentler this time. \"Sleep well.\"","She left, closing the door behind her with a quiet click. The teasing presence went with her. Rael stood alone in the small room for a moment, listening to the muffled sound of the common room below and the occasional footstep in the hall. The earlier heat under his skin slowly settled. He still did not fully understand why Elena and now Mira—both older, both carrying that calm, finished quality of women who had already grown into themselves—were the ones who pulled at the new, inconvenient awareness in him. Puberty was the easy answer. It was also the only one he had.","He sat on the edge of the bed, then lay back carefully, feeling the accumulated ache of the day’s fights in his shoulders and ribs. The ceiling was plain wood. The mana lamp on the table gave a steady, low light. Outside, Aetherion’s long night was fully in place.","It was time to look at the numbers.","Rael opened his status.","╔══════════ STATUS ═════════╗","Name: Rael","Race: Human","Level: 14 -> 15","Tier: Awakened","Main Class: Analyze (E-Rank), Infernomancer (B-Rank)","Copied Class: FrostHeart (A-Rank) (20%)","Bloodline: Abyssal Sovereign","Stage: Dormant","Effect: +100% Growth Multiplier","━━━━━━━━ ATTRIBUTES ━━━━━━━━","Strength: 44 -> 46","Agility: 50 -> 52","Mana: 48 -> 50","Vitality: 44 -> 46","Intelligence: 11 -> 12 (Sealed)","Unallocated Stats: 10","━━━━━━━━ RESOURCES ━━━━━━━━━","Souls: 88 -> 244","HP: 46 / 46","━━━━━━━━ SKILLS ━━━━━━━━━━━","Inferno Bolt (Expert), Hellfire (Expert), Flame Manipulation (Master), Frost Bolt (Novice) , Ice Armor (Apprentice), Frost Manipulation (Novice)","━━━━━━ BLOODLINE SKILLS ━━━━━━━","Conceal (Grandmaster)","╚═════════════════════════╝","He saw the level up even after killing the pack of fang wolfs and even an alpha he was still level up only by 1 it shows that as much as you level up it becomes harder to level up. Atleast his stats increase double compared to other people as every attributes of his increase by two who he levels up and he knows the common knowledge is that you only get one additional stats when you level up. It’s because of his bloodline he is becoming stronger at a double rate.","He could say even though his Tier is Awakened he could now easily beat anyone from his tier and even put a good fight to an average Awakened+ if he wants to. But it’s all in the theory nothing is sure until he tests it in real life. Rael lied back on the bed and closed his eyes and tried to sleep in this new world for the first time.","---"]},{"number":75,"title":"Chapter 75: Ekaksha [1]","paragraphs":["[Important Chapter]","Rael was too tired to fight the sleep that came for him. The bed at the inn was plain, the room quiet except for the distant murmur of the common room below, and the accumulated weight of the day’s fights settled into his limbs like lead. His eyes closed. For a moment there was only ordinary darkness.","Then the sound of chains.","Not the rattle of metal against stone or the clink of something being dragged. This was deeper—old iron under tension, a low, resonant shudder that seemed to come from everywhere at once. He had known that sound for as long as he had known his own name. It was not the first time he had heard it in the space between waking and true sleep. It would not be the last.","He opened his eyes.","He stood on a surface of still, clear water that stretched to every horizon. The water was only ankle-deep where he stood, perfectly transparent, yet beneath it the depths fell away into an endless, lightless blue. Every small shift of his weight sent thin ripples outward that traveled farther than they should have before fading. Above him the sky was a clean, cloud-softened blue, brighter and more open than any sky in Aetherion or on Titan. There was no sun, only even light without source.","He knew this place.","It had been appearing in his sleep since he was small enough that the memory of the first visit sat more in feeling than in clear images. The water. The sky. The quiet. And the presence that always waited here.","The chains sounded again behind him.","Rael turned.","The figure hung in the air some distance away, suspended by seven heavy chains that ran outward in different directions—into the sky, into the water, into places that did not seem to exist in ordinary space. Both arms were pulled wide. The legs were bound. One chain crossed the hips. Another circled the throat. The body was large, larger than any human Rael had stood beside, and covered in old, pale scars that stood out against skin that looked drained of blood. A length of worn white cloth wrapped the lower body and part of the chest. There was no face—only a smooth, featureless plane where eyes, nose, and mouth should have been.","Until lips formed.","They appeared as if the idea of a mouth had simply decided to exist. The voice that came out was low, rough, and heavy with age.","\"Hello, kid.\"","Rael regarded the chained figure without surprise or fear. The indifference on his face was the kind that only long familiarity could produce.","\"Hello, Ekaksha.\"","This was the oldest and least answerable mystery of his life.","He still did not know whether the place was a dream in the ordinary sense, a space inside his own mind, or something that touched him from elsewhere. He did not know whether the figure in the chains was a real entity, a fragment of something older, or a shape his sleeping mind had built and refused to take apart. As a child he had been terrified of it. The facelessness, the chains, the way the water never quite behaved like water—everything about it had sat wrong. He had told his mother. He had told other members of the family when the dreams kept returning. No one believed him. They spoke the way adults speak to children who have nightmares: it was only a dream, it would pass, he should not dwell on it. They said these things as if he did not already understand what ordinary dreams felt like. This was different, and he had known it even then.","At some point—he could not have named the exact year—fear had worn down into something closer to acceptance. The figure never attacked him. It never tried to leave the chains. It simply remained, suspended and silent, until Rael learned to sit near it and speak. When he had asked for a name, the entity had said it no longer remembered who or what it had been. Rael had given it one instead: Ekaksha. One-eye. The entity had accepted the name without comment, and over time the word had settled into place between them like a shared fact.","After the awakening, the visits had stopped for a long stretch. Rael had wondered, in the quieter moments between the First Realm and the return to Titan, whether that part of him had been severed along with so much else. Seeing the chained figure again now, solid and present under the sourceless sky, closed a gap he had not fully admitted was open.","\"You’re back,\" Rael said. \"I thought I might have lost you after the awakening. It’s been a while.\"","Ekaksha’s single eye opened. It appeared the same way the mouth had—suddenly there, set in the center of the otherwise blank face, pale and depthless.","\"You say that as if I went somewhere.\"","\"You did.\"","\"I was always here.\"","Rael walked closer across the still water and sat down a few feet from the suspended figure. The surface barely rippled.","\"Still feels like you disappeared.\"","A faint movement at the edge of the formed lips might have been a smile.","\"It’s been about a year since you showed up this clearly,\" Rael said.","\"You’ve grown.\"","\"No shit. It’s been a year.\"","\"You used to be shorter.\"","Rael glanced up at him. \"That’s your observation?\"","\"You were annoying even then.\"","A short breath of laughter left Rael before he could stop it. \"Funny. I remember you being the quiet one.\"","\"I still am.\"","\"You’re talking right now.\"","\"Then perhaps you are the reason, kid.\"","Rael leaned back on his hands and looked at the chains. They did not move, yet the sense of immense, locked weight never left them. For a while neither of them spoke. The silence was not awkward. It never had been. It was simply the shape of the space they shared.","He had known Ekaksha for as long as he could form lasting memories. In the early years the questions had poured out of him without restraint. Who are you? Why are you here? Who put the chains on you? Why can’t I leave until I wake? Why can I see you when no one else believes you exist? Ekaksha had never given proper answers. Sometimes there was silence. Sometimes there was a reply so sideways it only created more questions. After enough years Rael had stopped demanding explanations. Not because the curiosity had died. He had simply learned that some answers could not be pulled out of the figure in the chains by force or repetition. They would come, or they would not.","What he did know—what he had tested in small, practical ways over childhood—was that Ekaksha’s presence had never been useless.","People had called him a genius when he was young. Teachers, tutors, even some of the sharper members of the Blackthorn household had used the word with varying degrees of surprise and calculation. Rael had never fully believed it. He was not dull; he could hold his own. But the leaps that others praised often arrived after he had sat with a problem in the quiet of this place and asked the chained figure a careful question. Training forms. Historical dates. The logic behind a mana circulation method he was not yet supposed to know. Ekaksha would speak, or indicate, or simply make the shape of the answer available in a way Rael still could not describe cleanly. Then Rael would wake and find that the difficult thing sat more clearly in his mind than it had the night before. That quiet, consistent help was the largest reason he had never turned against the presence inside his sleep. Fear had faded. Resentment had never really formed. Gratitude, of a complicated and unspoken kind, had taken their place.","He still had theories. The figure might be a sealed remnant of something older than the current age. It might be a piece of his own bloodline that the rest of the family did not carry, or did not speak of. It might be an external entity that had attached itself to him for reasons that had nothing to do with the Blackthorn name. Without confirmation from Ekaksha, every theory remained only a shape in the dark. Rael had learned to live with the uncertainty the same way he had learned to live with the sealed Intelligence on his status screen—acknowledge the limit, keep moving, wait for the moment the limit shifted.","The water under him remained still except for the small disturbances of his own breathing. The chains held their tension. The single eye watched him without blinking.","\"You’ve been busy,\" Ekaksha said after a long stretch of quiet. The voice was the same rough, distant thing it had always been. \"The world outside this place has left marks on you.\"","Rael did not deny it. \"A few.\"","\"And you are still asking the wrong questions when you are awake.\"","\"Probably.\" Rael let his head tip back toward the empty blue sky. \"You planning on answering any of the right ones tonight?\"","The formed lips moved in that almost-smile again.","\"Not yet, kid.\"","It was the answer Rael had expected. It was the answer he had received, in one form or another, for as long as he had been coming here. He accepted it the way he accepted the weight of the chains and the depth under the water—not with satisfaction, but with the calm of long practice.","---"]}]);
